{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire","id":1515462,"title":"Conspiracy Theory: Tending the Fire","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-18T10:32:12-07:00","id":1515462,"title":"Conspiracy Theory: Tending the Fire","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Me: *working on my new M:tAw homebrew*<br /> <br /> <i>knock on the door</i><br /> <br /> Me: &quot;Come in!&quot;<br /> <br /> <i>door busts open</i><br /> <br /> Hunter: &quot;How dare you!&quot; *throws Tending the Fire right to my face*<br /> <br /> Me: *falling over to the floor*<br /> <br /> <i>moment of silence</i><br /> <br /> Me: *getting up* &quot;Fine, fine, I'll talk about the companion..&quot;<br /> <br /> So, overall, Tending the Fire has a lot of good things in it. The Social currency system is \\really cool, and a lot of the new orgs are very interesting. However, there is a bit of an.. issue. It could be the wordcount confinement, some external pressure or something else, but some parts of the book feel very rough around the edges. This mostly impacts, imo, the conspiracies- most compacts manage to maintain a cohesive and strong theme through their writeup even with the limited wordcount (well, with one notable exception... Yes I'm looking at you Elden Covenant. No wonder you are on the brink of breaking into two compacts- you <i>are</i> two compacts). Conspiracies, on the other hand, feel like they got the short end of the stick. While some manage to do very well even with their limited wordcount, some feel more like compacts than conspiracies, some feel very conceptual, and some have some big question marks about how they operate or even exists. For example, I <i>love</i> the Survivors Club, and I <i>love</i> their Endowments, but their presentation and name <i>really</i> makes them feel like a compact- and believe me, names are important to make sure whether a group feels like a compact or a conspiracy. Also, as we have explored before in this series (i<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473351-conspiracy-theory-hub-thread\">n case you missed it</a>), conspiracies generally tend to play on RL conspiracy theories and shadowy organizations. The Catholic Church, the Illuminati, the Templars, the Hashashins, Zaibatsu, Gnostics, Satanic Panic, Men in Black... everything is all about that paranoia and turning it on its head, while still remaining true to the conspiracy roots. Sure, not every conspiracy plays on the same themes to the same level, but it feels like most of the conspiracies in TtF neglect that theme all together. Again, let me be clear- this is not a bad book and I enjoy a lot of it, but the places it <i>in my opinion</i> fails are just very frustrating. It makes me feel like I need to put a lot of job to make some of those groups playable (and together with the fact that most of the conspiracies get around 5 Endowments it also extends to the mechanics), which is not what I hoped it would be.<br /> <br /> But since when I am not just throwing wordcount on Hunter for no good reason?<br /> <br /> So yeah, generally this series was about analyzing the <i>LOOOOOORE</i> that the different conspiracies have and present some wacky theory about what is their true agenda and goals, and how do they seek to change the world. The problem is that those conspiracies, well, don't really have lore- again, limited wordcount and other reasons, basically they are all very straightforward. As such, in here I am going to do some heavy rebuilding instead- well, depending on the conspiracy, some will not need it as much as others. Basically, I will present my own personal take, what are my issues, and I try to resolve them, by using the same paradigm I've used to define conspiracies earlier. I hope that it would also help other people who had issues with the writeups- and at worst case scenarios, it would help me rebuilding the conspiracies into something which is useful to me, and isn't it what truly important in homebrew? :P<br /> <br /> Anyway, Conspiracy Theory season 2- here we go!</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Asclepion</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">My personal favorite conspiracy from TtF, I think that Asclepion manages to preform the best among all of the new conspiracies. Name? Check. Strange origin? Check. Filling a missing niche in the Vigil? Check. Overall, Asclepion do well what they are meant to do- a secretive organization dedicated for saving human lives in the Vigil, without needing to pay tons of money to Cheiron Health Insurance. It is clear where do they come from, why do they have Endowments, what their Endowments are meant to be, what is their Vigil and how their Endowments work to help their Vigil. Overall, very solid writeup, very good, A+. As such, unlike the other conspiracies in the book, I don't feel like I need to do some heavy rebuilding to the conspiracy and that I have enough lore to actually start and piece out what this conspiracy is </span><i>really</i><span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\"> all about- because let me tell you, the writeup may give us a lot about the conspiracy, but it also opens up more questions- and in a good way.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Let's start with what we know- the (modern) version of Asclepion is very young, being originated around WW2. They are also, unsurprisingly, Greek, and we know that they had an alliance with the AKD which helped them to become a conspiracy. Their Endowments also seem to be connected to the hero-god Asklepios- or, at least, the a temple dedicated to the deity. They hold a religious undertone which is very dominant within the conspiracy, with one of the factions being dedicated to the mystical angle. As for the Endowments themselves, we know that gaining them requires some kind of meditation/detachment from reality, religious symbolism in relation for healing (be it deities, sacred jewelry or something along those lines), and water- and that those who leave the conspiracy slowly lose their Endowments. This already give us some strong hints about the nature of their Endowments- that it is religious, related to water, disconnects the hunter from reality and is a collective act. Also, can we just note what a miss it is that their logo does not include the Rod of Asclepius? I mean,. come on! Low hanging fruit, guys!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Aaaaaaanyway, a sort research quickly explains a lot of what we see about Enkoimesis- its name basically refers to a ancient healing practice used by the ancient temples of Asclepios , which also involved dream incubation as part of the ritual alongside the use of opium and similar materials during a 'temple sleep'- which are the circumstances under which the Endowment was discovered during WW2. Basically, Enkiomesis is very straightforward- it is the treatment that the ancient priests have used to heal their patients. The water symbolism, again, is nothing extraordinary- it is a tool for purification, such as the healing baths used by the temples during ancient Greece. Overall, nothing special here. Sure, the weird thing is that it works, but there are documents that show that the temples seem to have actually being not bad in saving lives in the time, and this if the CofD, so magic does work and rituals have power. Nothing weird in here, then. Just weird magic doing weird magic stuff.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Except...</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Noticed something strange in this paragraph? Enkiomesis is a ancient method used to </span><i>heal the </i><i>patients</i><span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">, not to initiate the priests. The ones who are sick should go through Enkiomesis, not the healers- and that means either one of two things: either in the CofD, the texts of the temples have been distorted, or, the more interesting option, that in order to heal others, the hunter themselves must first be </span><i>healed</i><span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\"> from.... something. From what, it is not clear yet- but just something to keep in mind that by joining the conspiracy, the hunter gets cleansed of something, and through that purification they are able to become the healers who would save life during the Vigil.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Now, it should be noted that it is really not clear whether Enkiomesis requires a &quot;recharge&quot; by going through the purification rite on a regular basis or that it needs only one time to get initiated. I am currently going with the second interpretation as the text does not explicitly mentions it is needed either in the setting material or in the mechanics, so it is an assumption that we are doing here- and which has implications. Why? Because if it requires a regular &quot;recharge&quot;, it would make sense about how people are losing their Endowments after leaving the conspiracy. However, if it is a one time initiation, something else is going on- something which is probably related to the whole dream incubation thing that those hunters have going for them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Basically, when you are talking about something along the line of &quot;dream incubation&quot; in the CofD, the immediate association is the Astral- the collective soul of humanity. That sense of collectivity and religious aspects all resonate very strongly with the Astral, as it seems that the hunters get detached from the mortal world and enter a meditative state. While the book does not explicitly mention it, Enkiomesis was used in 2 different manners by the priests- either as a replacement of anesthesia or (the case which is more relevant considering the whole dream incubation thing) to be visited by Asclepios or gain visions from the god, which were later interoperated by the priests. Taking this into account, it seems that the process of the dream incubation is meant to commune with the force which the conspiracy calls &quot;Asklepios&quot; and gain their blessings- which makes Asklepios, in this instance, a symbol or manifestation of collective healing. We also know that this entity does not depend on the collective in order to exist, as the temples of Asklepios were abandoned for quite a while (hence why the conspiracy is young), yet we need to assume that the origins of Asclepion are much older considering the history established behind the practices the conspiracy uses. Considering the unstable nature of the Astral and how well it is shaped by humanity, it makes me think that maybe some ancient Astral entity which serves as the collective perception of healing may not be the best candidate. It should be something more... stable. More symbolic. Supernal, perhaps? Asklepios as the Supernal symbol of healing may actually fit the conspiracy, especially with the whole religious and mystical practices, but then we have the little problem of the Abyss being a gashing and horrifying ocean which breaks the Supernal from the Fallen. You know, a small issue, nothing of importance- but it does make you wonder: if Asklepios heals the initiate from something, and Asklepios is a Supernal entity, could it be that they heal the hunters from the Curse of Sleep? Something to think about.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Anyway, whether we are talking about an Astral collective or a Supernal symbol, this may solve the Endowments of the conspiracy- but not the conspiracy itself. After all, there is something.. funky going on in here. The AKD allying with the early Asclepion? Why? I mean, sure &quot;we are all on team humanity&quot; and all of that nonsense- but let's be serious for a second. Conspiracies don't like one another. Conspiracies are very jealous for their secrets. Having a young conspiracy in your home base is the perfect opportunity to assimilate them into your structure and steal their secrets for yourself. Heck, their bases are found in the heart of the AKD's control- even though it is worth to mention that Greece is, om ,many ways, the &quot;cradle of conspiracies&quot;. Apparently that's what happens when you have way too many mystery cults. The AKD, Cheiron, the Promethean Brotherhood and maybe even the Cainite Heresy could track their origin to the region- with Cheiron being a yet very important conspiracy in that context. After all, Cheiron is not only another conspiracy of Greek origin that has connection to medicine, but Asclepios in mythology was the student of Chiron, from which he learned the art of healing. However, where Cheiron went to a more &quot;scientific&quot; methodology, Asclepion is much more religious in nature. Still, even if we are talking about it from a modern POV, we still have a strong issue in modern times- Cheiron is a business, after all, and what Asclepion offers is, in fact, a way to ruin the pharmacy business. The very risk should make the Board of Directories to at least move uncomfortably in their chairs. Kinda makes you think about how in mythology Hades requested Asclepios's death as he feared he would rob him from all of the souls as he was just so damn good in his job. Maybe something worthy to put a pin on.<br /> <br /> It should be noted that a alliance between conspiracies is not unheard of- even though it is still relatively rare. For example, we do know that VASCU and TFV do use similar terms, which shows that at least some cooperation took place while VASCU was still part of the government. A more clear example, however, comes in the from of the MM and the Lucifuge- where, again, we have 2 conspiracies who are very close to each other geographically and that have some very close connection as their leaders are in very good relationship. From this we may deduce what are the requirements for two conspiracies to actually work together- first, common theology (both the Lucifuge and the MM are Catholic and agree on the origin of evil), personal attachments (the Lady and Baudolino), and agreement on the goal (punch Satan in the face). Sure, they may disagree on a lot of things, but as long as the MM can accept the existence of the Lucifuge and have a place for them as &quot;not immediately evil&quot; and the Lucifuge remain Catholic enough (or at least Christian), they conspiracies can work together. As a result, we can learn a lot about Asclepion by learning from the closest ally, the AKD.</span><br /> <br /> The main thing that we know about the AKD theology is that it is Minoan- the Labyrinths, the Mistress of Honey and Asterion, the Flood, all of those are very Minoan in essence. We also know that they are based in Athens, and that the story about the Minotaur is viewed as a preservation of an historic conflict between the ancient Mycenean and the Minoans, which ended with Mycenean victory, so we have some assimilation of cultures here. Anyway, we can fully agree that the AKD are not Monotheistic, and likely are related to Greek mystery cults. Basically, the AKD are pagans, and that already provides a common frame for them to work with Asclepion. The fact that Asclepion does not employ relics in their rituals already means that they do not fight against one another. However, the fact that Asclepion does not target monsters who are not violent towards humans in general kinda puts them on less stable ground with the AKD, who targets witches. However, there is something else that we need to remember about the AKD- they are obsessed with the past. They collect relics, preserve texts, archive ancient treasures of the past- and, in a way, Asclepion is a living relic. They are a piece of uncovered history of Greek origin, of the pagan era. They are precious in the same manner that every relic is precious- they are a piece of Greek history, and as such part of humanity's inheritance- and as such, should be &quot;owned&quot; and protected by the AKD. This also means protecting the practice from the unworthy hands- the AKD probably hate themselves for letting something as dangerous as the Rite of Hecate to run around, not to mention the thought of the practice falling to the hands of witches, or witches infiltrating the conspiracy. They needed guidance to avoid compromise. They needed to be protected, like any relic does. And so, the AKD worked hard to make sure that Asclepion will not fall again.<br /> <br /> But why did they fall in the first time?<br /> <br /> Well, the easy answer is probably Christianity. I mean, the Therapuetae of Asclepios are probably the historical origin of the conspiracy, being the healer cult of Asclepios in the ancient era. The short lived Cult of Glycon and the College of Asclepios and Hygeia may also have been reincarnations of the conspiracy. Still, Christianity is known to adapt things to its arsenal when needed- the Aves Minerva have became the Cainite Heresy, for example, and pagan organizations have survived the ancient period. Sure, many have also died out, so it is nothing too weird- but we are in a Conspiracy Theory thread, which means that things are always murky and we take no easy answers. Heck, such healing rituals could be very useful for Christian hunters, and the Rod and Snakes of Asclepios are, well, everywhere! No, something killed the conspiracy, and it wasn't Christianity.<br /> <br /> I claim it was Cheiron.<br /> <br /> Again, Cheiron are also a healer cult of Greek origin- which means that they compete over the same niche. However, for Cheiron healing means business, while for Ascleipon healing means religion. Cheiron sew the pieces of monsters together to heal their subjects and create heroes, Asclepion cleanse their patients from all supernatural effect. Cheiron seeks to control. Asclepion seeks to provide. The two organizations are, by nature, hostile to one another- but they are also strongly related to one another. Asclepios was a student of Chiron- so what if it is true in thsi case as well? What if the Asclepion have splintered from Chieron long time ago? What if Asklepion is in fact related to Cheiron- maybe it is a rogue member of the Board of Directors? Maybe it is a Deviant created by the early conspiracy, or even a &quot;finished product&quot;, a person cured from the Contagion and that now works to cleanse all of humanity? Maybe he was a witch? Cheiron are rumored to have worked with those after all. It could be that Asclepios was a mage student of the powers behind Cheiron who Ascended through the art of healing? Could explain why we see his symbols <i>everywhere</i>. Or perhaps Enkiomesis is the magic behind Thaumatech, the ritual that allows unnatural flesh to bind together against all reason- a specific application of a wider system. Cheiron may have taught him the art of healing as part of a trade, yet Asclepios have abused his arts, saving too many souls, causing imbalance. Death is a trade as well- this is what Cheiron knows and Asclepios ignores. Hades is both the Lord of the Dead and the King of the Treasures Below. Asclepios violated the trade between Life and Death- and if he was a mage, that means violating the very symbols of Tyranny represented by the Chancellor. This was his challenge for Ascension- and Cheiron, from their point of view, might have seen it as a threat to the world. And agreement was reached, the Pax Arcana was preserved. Asclepios Ascended- but his organization was to be destroyed. Trade must be honored, and the Therapuetae have violated it by saving too many lives.<br /> <br /> And that means that even in modern times, the Asclepion is destined to meet their enemies once more- yet where they are still building their power, Cheiron are the movers and shakers of the world. And the only thing they have by their side is their devotion, alliance with the KAD- and the power of a god. Still not enough to defeat capitalism imo, but hey what do I know.<br /> <br /> Anyway- let's sum it up. Asklepios was a student of the powers behind Cheiron during ancient Greece, probably an Awakened but not necessary, who managed to exceed the expectations of Cheiron by generalizing the forces which allow Thaumatech to work into a wider system, and then used the same system in order to become a symbol of healing and Ascend simply because the Chancellor could not have accepted his existence in the Fallen. His arts were so useful that when they spread across the ancient world, they saved so many lived that the balance of the world was at risk, allowing the Contagion to start sipping through the cracks. Cheiron, finding the act as against their mission and a disruption of the &quot;laws of trade&quot;, acted to destroy the cult in the first opportunity they had. Millenia later, WW2 happens, bombs drop, the ancient secret is uncovered, Asklepios returns and moves again in order to manage and achieve his goal... healing the world.<br /> <br /> From what?<br /> <br /> Again, this is the main issue that we have. From what the initiation heals people? The Contagion? Could make sense but also a lazy answer, as Cheiron are already doing it (at least, according to the theory- a CONSPIRACY THEORY). But if Asklepion is indeed an Ascended being as I claim, the answer should indeed be the Curse of Sleep. He can't provide Awakening- as he is not a Watchtower and has no control over them. However, as long as members of the conspiracy are part of his symbol through the initiation ritual, then they are cursed from Sleep. His goal, in that case, would be to heal the world- the first step was becoming the symbol of healing, the second is to allow that symbol of heal people from the curses and illnesses of the world, the third should be to envelope the whole world under his own symbol. The endgame should probably, in that case, to heal the gaping wound in the fabric of the multiverse- the Abyss itself. This, in turn, would provide Ascension to the world- creating a world without diseases, without pain, without death. The Underworld and the Depths below it would be gone, the tyrants of Heaven would fall from their thrones, the Contagion would die out. A new world, a healthy world, a perfect world. A world where the Halls of hades are empty and humanity is free from pain. Not a new Atlantis- but something greater. Obviously, that means that <i>a lot</i> of entities will be against this vision- the Exarchs, the Abyss, Inferno, the Chthonic Gods, the Contagion, the Board of Directors, the Judges. The list goes on. That means that the followers of Asklepios would have to tackle each of those, one by one, removing those flaws from the world. Sure, it would take time- but what is a time for a Ascended being?<br /> <br /> Or, maybe, there is a different way- perhaps Asklepios's whole plan is to instigate a War in Heaven by having his cult creating the imbalance of souls. Getting the Chthonic gods mad, the Judges mad, Ammut mad, Inferno mad. Too many are alive, not enough die. The ancient laws are not being obeyed, the Pax Arcana being violated- and from that chaos, maybe, just maybe, the new world will be forged, as long as Aklepios would win. Otherwise... well let's ignore that possibility. But Cheiron doesn't ignore. They can't ignore. The ancient trade must be protected- and if that means fighting their own disci0ple, so be it.<br /> <br /> Or that Cheiron are just capitalistic bastards who wants monopoly over life and death by themselves- <i>but where is the fun in that?</i><br /> <br /> *********************<br /> <br /> Well, that's it for the first entry. Next we'll have Campion Wildlife Services- and this is where we are going to start move much more into the &quot;rebuilding&quot; territory. Hope you'll like it!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T10:42:36-07:00","id":1515465,"title":"","content":"The list of Conspiracies with monsters exerting control or influence on them is gonna be fun to expand when I finish up TtF (White Hare Society and Endowments remaining!)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T15:35:00-07:00","id":1515489,"title":"","content":"Also something I missed- the name of the founder, Konstantina Svolou, has the same surname as Maria Svolou and Alexander Svolos, a married couple who served as political leadership among socialist movements, feminism and resistance to the Axis powers during WW2- which is when Asclepion was founded. This both gives the AKD another reason to support them (anti-Axis alliance) and gives some more interesting facts we may learn about their ideology, as Konstantina is (in the CofD) likely related to the couple. As such, it is likely that the conspiracy has some socialists undertones and philosophy- that's probably why they just give healthcare to everyone! Those communists! :P<br /> <br /> This also puts them in a direct clash with Cheiron, as if the conspiracy still maintains those socialist values, well, let's say they and Cheiron are not going to get along together.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T15:52:54-07:00","id":1515491,"title":"","content":"While I understand the chancellor would not like them, wouldn't their goal put them against the Arcanum of death too? While the Awakened nation is expected to be able to manipulate all of these things, it doesn't mean they have no place in the world in terms of natural progression. I feel like moros mages would not be comfortable with the ascended mage's plans. After all, Death is so the living can move on from that which is holding them back. Even the Obrimos feel there is a process for the soul after Death. It's only an inferior Arcanum due to its sudden absence at odds with the ever present mechanics of the universe","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T15:53:15-07:00","id":1515492,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515489#post1515489\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also something I missed- the name of the founder, Konstantina Svolou, has the same surname as Maria Svolou and Alexander Svolos, a married couple who served as political leadership among socialist movements, feminism and resistance to the Axis powers during WW2- which is when Asclepion was founded. This both gives the AKD another reason to support them (anti-Axis alliance) and gives some more interesting facts we may learn about their ideology, as Konstantina is (in the CofD) likely related to the couple. As such, it is likely that the conspiracy has some socialists undertones and philosophy- that's probably why they just give healthcare to everyone! Those communists! :P<br /> <br /> This also puts them in a direct clash with Cheiron, as if the conspiracy still maintains those socialist values, well, let's say they and Cheiron are not going to get along together.</div> </div> </div> </div>An idle thing I haven't been able to directly follow up on yet-Across Seven Wonders does a lot establish what the fairy presence is like in the Grecian states, and I seem to recall a mention about the presence of the Order of the Onierophysics in that Dark Era, a First Edition Entitlement whose schtick is <i>very</i> similar to Asclepion.<br /> <br /> That's pretty barebones, and like I said I haven't had the time to really cross check all of that, but I found that interesting to have crop up in my head.<br /> <br /> (and to tie it back into your post here, changeling Freeholds are pretty likely to have socialist/communist undertones no matter how classist they can otherwise get due to how little any given returnee has and the fairly easy to draw line between established members with the sympathy of &quot;if only I had a hand&quot; affecting their politics, so if there is a fairy/changeling influence on Asclepion, that would likely reinforce their political stances)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T16:08:45-07:00","id":1515493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Primordial newcomer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515491#post1515491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I understand the chancellor would not like them, wouldn't their goal put them against the Arcanum of death too? While the Awakened nation is expected to be able to manipulate all of these things, it doesn't mean they have no place in the world in terms of natural progression. I feel like moros mages would not be comfortable with the ascended mage's plans. After all, Death is so the living can move on from that which is holding them back. Even the Obrimos feel there is a process for the soul after Death. It's only an inferior Arcanum due to its sudden absence at odds with the ever present mechanics of the universe</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably true- even though &quot;Death as a Change&quot; may still exist in the world that Asclepion try to create and I think that this is something which the Death Arcana mostly represents (meaning, Death is not the end). Like, Time Lord regeneration is still &quot;death&quot;, but you are still alive- but they would still create the imbalance of souls that Asklepios (whatever it may be) tries to bring in order to create a world without death. And it may very well be that it means the complete removal of Death as a concept. Just add more entities who would be against those efforts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">An idle thing I haven't been able to directly follow up on yet-Across Seven Wonders does a lot establish what the fairy presence is like in the Grecian states, and I seem to recall a mention about the presence of the Order of the Onierophysics in that Dark Era, a First Edition Entitlement whose schtick is <i>very</i> similar to Asclepion.<br /> <br /> That's pretty barebones, and like I said I haven't had the time to really cross check all of that, but I found that interesting to have crop up in my head.<br /> <br /> (and to tie it back into your post here, changeling Freeholds are pretty likely to have socialist/communist undertones no matter how classist they can otherwise get due to how little any given returnee has and the fairly easy to draw line between established members with the sympathy of &quot;if only I had a hand&quot; affecting their politics, so if there is a fairy/changeling influence on Asclepion, that would likely reinforce their political stances)\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>well, that's interesting! I should also give it a look. I have no objection for Asklepios being a Fae entity (even though the companion does suggest the possibility of Court being formed around godlike entities and not concepts) or even the &quot;concept of Healing&quot;. The whole Court systems and bargains and such could be used easily to explain why people who enter the conspiracy gain access to the Endowment and lose it if they leave it (entering and breaching a contract), so the communal magical system works here, as well as the whole &quot;dream incubation&quot; thing. Would also fit the idea of diverging from Cheiron, as I have previously established how the powers behind Cheiron really care about trade, so a Changeling/similar fey entity making a deal to learn healing from them only to go rogue and try to create the &quot;world without death&quot; sounds like a very cool take imo.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T16:29:56-07:00","id":1515499,"title":"","content":"Gotcha. They are by far the most solid conspiracy in the book, and you did a great job explaining the AKD connection. Even their end goals you have for them tie together. I personally didn't get why the AKD would just let them go, but I do like your explanation as being more like under protection. Even then, there has to be envy. The AKD, especially the Sword, wants its magic back.<br /> <br /> I just wonder what skeletons (oh the irony) the healers may have. It's easy to believe they really are just that idealistic, but makes you wonder","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T17:57:45-07:00","id":1515503,"title":"","content":"Okay, THe Onierophysics don't feature at all in The Seven Wonders, so that connection to Greece is only extant on fairies getting a regionally relevant Dark Era, which doesn't mean anything.<br /> <br /> Still, without being able to read my copy of Lords of Summer at the moment, The Oneirophysics feel damnedably similar to Asclepion, and there's a question of how the metaphysic Enkiomesis is tied to the organization itself, since it's called out as a feature that disappears eventually as a Healer leaves the organization.<br /> <br /> Also, even though I have the Seven Wonders bridge burned, I still can't shake a feeling that the Oneirophysics are tied to Greece somehow. I'll have to check after I get everything back into the basement.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T13:29:30-07:00","id":1515555,"title":"","content":"So I read the Onierophysics entry from Lords of Summer and indeed there is a lot of parallels between their healing method and the ones provided by the historical Therapuetae (specifically the whole &quot;dream incubation&quot; thing), so there is likelihood that the two are related in some manner. The question is whether or not Asklepios is a changeling/fey entity or not. We do know, for example, the Merlin, while being a Awakened, is also claimed to have been a cambion- so it could be that Asklepios have also been a half-dream creature that have discovered and taught the secrets of healing to the local Lost community, and later have been Awakened (there reason I push for that angle is because that in that context there are strong indications of an Ascension like process as the dude really became the symbol for healing), and it also provides some motivation to heal something from the initiates (which is required imo). I also still think that him learning the arts of healing from Cheiron is required regardless of origin due to the mythical connotations that are established, and I feel will be a waste if they are not used. Again, that's just my angle, people are free to have their own theories obviously.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T14:25:14-07:00","id":1515566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515555#post1515555\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I read the Onierophysics entry from Lords of Summer and indeed there is a lot of parallels between their healing method and the ones provided by the historical Therapuetae (specifically the whole &quot;dream incubation&quot; thing), so there is likelihood that the two are related in some manner. The question is whether or not Asklepios is a changeling/fey entity or not. We do know, for example, the Merlin, while being a Awakened, is also claimed to have been a cambion- so it could be that Asklepios have also been a half-dream creature that have discovered and taught the secrets of healing to the local Lost community, and later have been Awakened (there reason I push for that angle is because that in that context there are strong indications of an Ascension like process as the dude really became the symbol for healing), and it also provides some motivation to heal something from the initiates (which is required imo). I also still think that him learning the arts of healing from Cheiron is required regardless of origin due to the mythical connotations that are established, and I feel will be a waste if they are not used. Again, that's just my angle, people are free to have their own theories obviously.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't particularly think it has to be a matter of either/or (the Cheiron argument being rooted in myth means that there's a whole of fuzziness in general), more than it suggests a particularly interesting soup of circumstances.<br /> <br /> That and, because my copy is in a mountain of books waiting to be moved back downstairs now that it's anti-flooded, those superficial similarities are gnawing at me for simply personal closure.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T16:35:28-07:00","id":1515583,"title":"","content":"Anyway, time for the next conspiracy in the list-<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Campion Wildlife Services</b></span><br /> <br /> Three reasons why you don't want to have your name in your conspiracy's.<br /> <br /> First- style. Seriously, you are building a brand here. People who join a conspiracy want to imagine that they are part of a grand structure, movers and shakers of culture. People who want to join a social club will just join a compact instead. You want them to put their life on the risk, after all- it is easier when you think you do it for some mythical goal, not for a pet project of a single person.<br /> <br /> Second- differentiation. Let's be honest, your organization is not the only one who does what it does. Even if you find some unique niche in the Vigil, you need to make sure that your brand will stand out of the crowd. Besides, you want the other movers and shakers to know that you are a big deal, not a dime in a dozens. When the only thing which differentiate you from the rest is the person who made the organization, no one is going to take yo seriously.<br /> <br /> Third - and most importantly - <i>you don't want to f-ing die</i>. I mean, you are now dealing with the most dangerous entities out there, and guess what? They are not stupid. You are going to have tons of enemies in the Vigil, and unless you are some occult force or keep some double identities or something, you don't want that monsters will associate your day job with your night job. That's why the Knights of St Adrian are not Howards Investigative Services- the company and the conspiracy are tied together, but are separated enough so their enemies will not immediately associate the company with the conspiracy. Putting your name in your conspiracy's is like putting a huge, red target on your head.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. Not a smart move Campion. You are one bad night from having a Deviant murdering you in your sleep.<br /> <br /> \u200bI don't want to be sound petty, but Campion Wildfire Services is, imo, not a really good name for a conspiracy- even though <i>it is</i> a good name for a compact, which is very localized and personalized. CWS suffer a lot from being too much of a compact than a conspiracy, and it shows in their writeup- it became nationwide on 2014 <i>somehow</i>, getting payed in order to deal with the roaming experiments done by the Web of Pain. We can assume that they got money by doing a good enough job at dealing with monstrous threats under the guise of them being &quot;rabid animals&quot; and such. I honestly don't know how much of a large business model it is, but let's assume that it is a good enough economic niche in the US. Id it enough to become a conspiracy? I don't know, but probably enough to become a nationwide company. I guess. Again, where I live there is one institute who is responsible for it, and it is more or less nationalized- but we also don't have the same amount of wilderness the US has. So again, I am just going to assume that this is a valid enterprise- but again, a conspiracy level structure? I think not.<br /> <br /> I should be noted that Campion is a very young organization- it has around 20 years at best, without the backing of secret government agencies, religion megastructures or occult societies. All of that, however, just puts <i>more</i> question marks in the whole business, not less. It really seems like while CWS is on the way to become a conspiracy, at the state it is presented it is not yet a conspiracy. It is found in a liminal place between being a conspiracy and being a compact, something which makes it very vulnerable considering that it may start biting on more than it could chew. Again, one bad night, and one of the Rangers getting caught in the wrong place doing the wrong thing to the wrong organization- and suddenly, the Web of Pain recognize the potential threat that Campion provides. Next day, the company is under lawsuit attacks. Day after, Dana is attacked, potentially killed. Soon after, the would be conspiracy is dissolved, getting absorbed into the Web of Pain. This is the likely scenario, as it seems.<br /> <br /> But what if it isn't?<br /> <br /> Let's assume, for example, that the entity sent to get rid of Dana messes up- Campion is still an hunter, she probably knows how to protect herself. Maybe she goes all Vigilante and starts a war in the Web of Pain- not a smart move, and it seems that Dana, in general, is not stupid. Perhaps she keeps it under cover, or even fakes her own death. The company goes through some restructure, maybe being called Wildlife Control and Services LLC or something along those lines, and Dana retreats from the day side and moves to the night side. Or maybe she does die, and someone else takes her place, learning the painful lessons from her demise.<br /> <br /> And maybe, just maybe, we are doing Dana Campion some large disservice.<br /> <br /> I mean, again- Campion Wildlife Services is a huge target on Dana's head, but we are talking about a former fog catcher- she knows a thing or two about <i>traps</i>, and every good trap needs a lore. We also know that she had time dealing with the Web of Pain, and probably got the guise of what is going on- shell companies within shell companies, false societies and fake religions, cults worming within business networks lurking within governmental bodies. The Web of Pain is a mess, and it is likely that Dana have understood the principle and used it for her own benefit. After all, an organization that hunts after Deviant horror is, in its essence, also a Deviant conspiracy, and it sounds likely that CWS have quickly adapted to its new situation. CWS is, probably, one face that the conspiracy has- the lure meant to draw the other organizations out, to see them as a &quot;cleanup company&quot; after their own mess. They know that in the case some of their experiments will escape and prove to be a security risk, they can use their own false aliases in order to hire CWS to get rid of the evidence, and as they are so out in the open they know exactly who they should contact and to which goal. They can keep the conspiracy under control because they know that Campion knows that they can get rid of them at any time- and so they will cooperate, no questions asked.<br /> <br /> They bite the lure, and the trap closes sharp upon them.<br /> <br /> Let's assume, for a second, that CWS is indeed only one face of a larger project- let's call it maybe Project: Lyssa (after the goddess of rabies), or maybe Catchfly (an alternative name for the Campion flower) or Silenus (the wine god from which the scientific name for the Campion flowers is derived). Whatever the name may be, it serves as the shadow of CWS- it collects the information about the clients, analyze their experiments, and plans methods- both legal and otherwise- to put an end to them. You use CWS in order to disrupt the Web, draw the spider, and then comes the wasp. Obviously, they should be discreet about it- the conspiracies should not connect that CWS that they use for their cleanup operations is in fact the same entity which later, say, bombs their laboratories, or files a lawsuit, or expose their crimes in the newspaper. Was Dana even the one who created the whole operation? Or could it be that the reason CWS rose to prominence in such a speed is because there was already an organization ready to leverage the opportunity CWS presented? Some older organization dedicated for fighting the Web of Pain, who found CWS of use by assimilating them into its already existing structure? And what if Dana was the one to actually uncover that organization, being the one who proposed that alliance knowing it would serve as a lure, for she is, indeed, a trapper who knows how to bring down her enemies?<br /> <br /> And all the while, the spider keeps spinning the Web, completely unware of the wasp watching from between the leaves.<br /> <br /> Ok, so we have established how CWS may be the forefront of Project: Lyssa by any other name- anything else we need to discuss?<br /> <br /> Ha, yes. Their.. Endowments...<br /> <br /> Like, I have some feeling towards Animal Control Kit, which are not very positive. Basic Field Kit is not even an Endowment- sure, it is a nice bonus to have, but it is not something that the conspiracy can grant and no one else can. Same for Mobile Transport Unit- it is just a secured ride, Cheiron have those by a dozen! As such, I dare to say that any part of the Animal Control Kit which is just a mundane bonus is not actually an Endowment. It is not unique, and just muddies the themes of the conspiracy. The rest of the Endowments- well, that's something we can work with. Monstrous abilities extracted and harvested from captured manticora (and, potentially, actual Deviants) is something that we can work with. The question is, in this case, how do they do this? Generally, it sounds pretty scientific- harvesting the organs that those monsters use and apply them into tools in the service of the Vigil. That's a bit Cheiron like in behavior, but it is an external factor. The thing is, that the source of power behind those monsters is generally the Divergence, not some actual biology. Sure, you could say that they can only harvest those abilities which are associated with a certain organ, but this is a boring answer and we are in a conspiracy theory thread. As such, what if CWS, or the strange organization we claim to stand behind them, have developed a method to harvest the Divergence, or at least its manifestations. Pseudo-science is the idea behind the Divergence after all, and finding a method to imbue regular objects with the power of stolen Variations sounds like something a conspiracy would do. Perhaps, in turn, that could lead us to the endgame of this conspiracy- a world where the Divergence is being under control, a world where there are no abnormalities in nature.<br /> <br /> A world without Pain.<br /> <br /> Yeah, it is very likely that dismantling the Web of Pain is the endgame of the conspiracy, but it is likely a step beyond it. They may want to take it apart and rebuild it, harvesting the corpses of the organizations and creating actual tools to fight the monsters in the dark. They weaponize the Divergence, but not by mutilating the soul of others- but by stealing the research of others and turning it into a stable methodology to fight the darkness. They want to introduce stability to the madness of the Divergence, and by doing so eliminate it from the world. After all, by unlocking the secrets for stabilizing Divergence, they may create a vaccine which would heal a cracked soul, a medicine which would remove the horrible modifications forced upon the innocents in the name of cooperate greed. Bring an end to the cycle of harm, and free the world from those who once tormented it once and for all.<br /> <br /> Rabies was once a deadly plague and a death sentence until the day Louis Pasteur brought the vaccines which changed the world forever. Who said that the Divergence can't be treated the same way? Or, at the very least, stopped preemptively, before the disease breaks out, before the soul cracks.<br /> <br /> The spider will be soon caught in its own Web- and the wasp would soon feast.<br /> <br /> **********************<br /> <br /> Ok, I did not expected for this to be my writeup for Campion- but here we are! I think I do like the conclusion I came to in this writeup, but let me know what do you think about it. It at least helps to rationalize to me a lot of things about CWS. Anyway, the next writeup will be about the Engimatics- and oh boy, do i have things to say about the Engimatics... see you next time!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T17:22:46-07:00","id":1515591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515583#post1515583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, time for the next conspiracy in the list-<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Campion Wildlife Services</b></span><br /> <br /> Three reasons why you don't want to have your name in your conspiracy's.<br /> <br /> First- style. Seriously, you are building a brand here. People who join a conspiracy want to imagine that they are part of a grand structure, movers and shakers of culture. People who want to join a social club will just join a compact instead. You want them to put their life on the risk, after all- it is easier when you think you do it for some mythical goal, not for a pet project of a single person.<br /> <br /> Second- differentiation. Let's be honest, your organization is not the only one who does what it does. Even if you find some unique niche in the Vigil, you need to make sure that your brand will stand out of the crowd. Besides, you want the other movers and shakers to know that you are a big deal, not a dime in a dozens. When the only thing which differentiate you from the rest is the person who made the organization, no one is going to take yo seriously.<br /> <br /> Third - and most importantly - <i>you don't want to f-ing die</i>. I mean, you are now dealing with the most dangerous entities out there, and guess what? They are not stupid. You are going to have tons of enemies in the Vigil, and unless you are some occult force or keep some double identities or something, you don't want that monsters will associate your day job with your night job. That's why the Knights of St Adrian are not Howards Investigative Services- the company and the conspiracy are tied together, but are separated enough so their enemies will not immediately associate the company with the conspiracy. Putting your name in your conspiracy's is like putting a huge, red target on your head.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. Not a smart move Campion. You are one bad night from having a Deviant murdering you in your sleep.<br /> <br /> \u200bI don't want to be sound petty, but Campion Wildfire Services is, imo, not a really good name for a conspiracy- even though <i>it is</i> a good name for a compact, which is very localized and personalized. CWS suffer a lot from being too much of a compact than a conspiracy, and it shows in their writeup- it became nationwide on 2014 <i>somehow</i>, getting payed in order to deal with the roaming experiments done by the Web of Pain. We can assume that they got money by doing a good enough job at dealing with monstrous threats under the guise of them being &quot;rabid animals&quot; and such. I honestly don't know how much of a large business model it is, but let's assume that it is a good enough economic niche in the US. Id it enough to become a conspiracy? I don't know, but probably enough to become a nationwide company. I guess. Again, where I live there is one institute who is responsible for it, and it is more or less nationalized- but we also don't have the same amount of wilderness the US has. So again, I am just going to assume that this is a valid enterprise- but again, a conspiracy level structure? I think not.<br /> <br /> I should be noted that Campion is a very young organization- it has around 20 years at best, without the backing of secret government agencies, religion megastructures or occult societies. All of that, however, just puts <i>more</i> question marks in the whole business, not less. It really seems like while CWS is on the way to become a conspiracy, at the state it is presented it is not yet a conspiracy. It is found in a liminal place between being a conspiracy and being a compact, something which makes it very vulnerable considering that it may start biting on more than it could chew. Again, one bad night, and one of the Rangers getting caught in the wrong place doing the wrong thing to the wrong organization- and suddenly, the Web of Pain recognize the potential threat that Campion provides. Next day, the company is under lawsuit attacks. Day after, Dana is attacked, potentially killed. Soon after, the would be conspiracy is dissolved, getting absorbed into the Web of Pain. This is the likely scenario, as it seems.<br /> <br /> But what if it isn't?<br /> <br /> Let's assume, for example, that the entity sent to get rid of Dana messes up- Campion is still an hunter, she probably knows how to protect herself. Maybe she goes all Vigilante and starts a war in the Web of Pain- not a smart move, and it seems that Dana, in general, is not stupid. Perhaps she keeps it under cover, or even fakes her own death. The company goes through some restructure, maybe being called Wildlife Control and Services LLC or something along those lines, and Dana retreats from the day side and moves to the night side. Or maybe she does die, and someone else takes her place, learning the painful lessons from her demise.<br /> <br /> And maybe, just maybe, we are doing Dana Campion some large disservice.<br /> <br /> I mean, again- Campion Wildlife Services is a huge target on Dana's head, but we are talking about a former fog catcher- she knows a thing or two about <i>traps</i>, and every good trap needs a lore. We also know that she had time dealing with the Web of Pain, and probably got the guise of what is going on- shell companies within shell companies, false societies and fake religions, cults worming within business networks lurking within governmental bodies. The Web of Pain is a mess, and it is likely that Dana have understood the principle and used it for her own benefit. After all, an organization that hunts after Deviant horror is, in its essence, also a Deviant conspiracy, and it sounds likely that CWS have quickly adapted to its new situation. CWS is, probably, one face that the conspiracy has- the lure meant to draw the other organizations out, to see them as a &quot;cleanup company&quot; after their own mess. They know that in the case some of their experiments will escape and prove to be a security risk, they can use their own false aliases in order to hire CWS to get rid of the evidence, and as they are so out in the open they know exactly who they should contact and to which goal. They can keep the conspiracy under control because they know that Campion knows that they can get rid of them at any time- and so they will cooperate, no questions asked.<br /> <br /> They bite the lure, and the trap closes sharp upon them.<br /> <br /> Let's assume, for a second, that CWS is indeed only one face of a larger project- let's call it maybe Project: Lyssa (after the goddess of rabies), or maybe Catchfly (an alternative name for the Campion flower) or Silenus (the wine god from which the scientific name for the Campion flowers is derived). Whatever the name may be, it serves as the shadow of CWS- it collects the information about the clients, analyze their experiments, and plans methods- both legal and otherwise- to put an end to them. You use CWS in order to disrupt the Web, draw the spider, and then comes the wasp. Obviously, they should be discreet about it- the conspiracies should not connect that CWS that they use for their cleanup operations is in fact the same entity which later, say, bombs their laboratories, or files a lawsuit, or expose their crimes in the newspaper. Was Dana even the one who created the whole operation? Or could it be that the reason CWS rose to prominence in such a speed is because there was already an organization ready to leverage the opportunity CWS presented? Some older organization dedicated for fighting the Web of Pain, who found CWS of use by assimilating them into its already existing structure? And what if Dana was the one to actually uncover that organization, being the one who proposed that alliance knowing it would serve as a lure, for she is, indeed, a trapper who knows how to bring down her enemies?<br /> <br /> And all the while, the spider keeps spinning the Web, completely unware of the wasp watching from between the leaves.<br /> <br /> Ok, so we have established how CWS may be the forefront of Project: Lyssa by any other name- anything else we need to discuss?<br /> <br /> Ha, yes. Their.. Endowments...<br /> <br /> Like, I have some feeling towards Animal Control Kit, which are not very positive. Basic Field Kit is not even an Endowment- sure, it is a nice bonus to have, but it is not something that the conspiracy can grant and no one else can. Same for Mobile Transport Unit- it is just a secured ride, Cheiron have those by a dozen! As such, I dare to say that any part of the Animal Control Kit which is just a mundane bonus is not actually an Endowment. It is not unique, and just muddies the themes of the conspiracy. The rest of the Endowments- well, that's something we can work with. Monstrous abilities extracted and harvested from captured manticora (and, potentially, actual Deviants) is something that we can work with. The question is, in this case, how do they do this? Generally, it sounds pretty scientific- harvesting the organs that those monsters use and apply them into tools in the service of the Vigil. That's a bit Cheiron like in behavior, but it is an external factor. The thing is, that the source of power behind those monsters is generally the Divergence, not some actual biology. Sure, you could say that they can only harvest those abilities which are associated with a certain organ, but this is a boring answer and we are in a conspiracy theory thread. As such, what if CWS, or the strange organization we claim to stand behind them, have developed a method to harvest the Divergence, or at least its manifestations. Pseudo-science is the idea behind the Divergence after all, and finding a method to imbue regular objects with the power of stolen Variations sounds like something a conspiracy would do. Perhaps, in turn, that could lead us to the endgame of this conspiracy- a world where the Divergence is being under control, a world where there are no abnormalities in nature.<br /> <br /> A world without Pain.<br /> <br /> Yeah, it is very likely that dismantling the Web of Pain is the endgame of the conspiracy, but it is likely a step beyond it. They may want to take it apart and rebuild it, harvesting the corpses of the organizations and creating actual tools to fight the monsters in the dark. They weaponize the Divergence, but not by mutilating the soul of others- but by stealing the research of others and turning it into a stable methodology to fight the darkness. They want to introduce stability to the madness of the Divergence, and by doing so eliminate it from the world. After all, by unlocking the secrets for stabilizing Divergence, they may create a vaccine which would heal a cracked soul, a medicine which would remove the horrible modifications forced upon the innocents in the name of cooperate greed. Bring an end to the cycle of harm, and free the world from those who once tormented it once and for all.<br /> <br /> Rabies was once a deadly plague and a death sentence until the day Louis Pasteur brought the vaccines which changed the world forever. Who said that the Divergence can't be treated the same way? Or, at the very least, stopped preemptively, before the disease breaks out, before the soul cracks.<br /> <br /> The spider will be soon caught in its own Web- and the wasp would soon feast.<br /> <br /> **********************<br /> <br /> Ok, I did not expected for this to be my writeup for Campion- but here we are! I think I do like the conclusion I came to in this writeup, but let me know what do you think about it. It at least helps to rationalize to me a lot of things about CWS. Anyway, the next writeup will be about the Engimatics- and oh boy, do i have things to say about the Engimatics... see you next time!</div> </div> </div> </div>Great job! I like how you turned it all around and made your criticisms of the compact into strengths. Your issues with the Endowment are definitely one I share.<br /> <br /> I do wish to expand on it to keep it more unique from chieron and TFV, but i do have a question. Isn't it odd that the Wildlife Services not only has the means to capture and contain these monsters safely, but also is able to extract their unique mutations and put them into technology? It's practically a bypass to avoid all the nasty issues of Divergence. Better yet, they don't even need to have their own resources from scratch. As you said, the conspiracy is a trap. Let the others in the Web of Pain risk it all on Divergence, let them use all those resources. When they mess up, swoop in, bag the monster, and make use of what you can out of them.<br /> <br /> And best of all, don't share the fact you've managed to stabilize these mutations separate from the main body. Really, nothing suspicious about that little discovery.<br /> <br /> Re-reading, i realize youve basically said the exact same thing concerning lore and oddities in their Endowment. I was just excited but it seems we had the same idea. I'd still like to spice up their Endowment to give it more gameplay oomph from TFV","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24173,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","user":"Primordial newcomer","date":". 06-19-2023, 10:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-20T14:44:17-07:00","id":1515692,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:20px\">The Engimatics</span><br /> <br /> Disclaimer- everything in this post is my own personal opinion. I am aware that not every group is for everyone and that the writing team have put a lot of thought around designing and writing the organizations in the book, under the confinements of what seems to be a very stressed wordcount. I am just someone who have written too much Hunter homebrew for their own good, and do not mean any disrespect to those who have invested time and mental resources in making the book. I am going to be as clear and constructive about my opinions here as possible, and if there are some &quot;harsh&quot; languages it is probably meant as a humoristic tool and is not intended against the writing team. I am also aware that there are people who do not share the same criticisms as I do and it is completely fine- it is a RPG game, and what works for one person may not work for another. I am judging this products according to my own needs and view about Hunter, that's all.<br /> <br /> So yeah, I assume that you could note hat if I have put that disclaimer up there, I may have some <i>strong</i> feelings about the Enigmatics.<br /> <br /> Note- I have no problem with the concept. Actually, I love the concept. This conspiracy is basically the Third Tier form of the Wilde Society from Dream Catchers, just without the fey connections. They are a group of artists who seek to be inspired by the supernatural and ends up making some cursed artwork- nothing bad in that! More than that, it is actually a good thing- between the tropes about &quot;mad artists&quot; and the corruption that takes place in the Arts Market, we really have a place for a conspiracy here. Artistic movements deal with some of the richest, most powerful people in the world, after all- it is not hard to imagine that there is something dark happening in the arts market in the CofD, and that a conspiracy may form from those who could use their art to inspire whatever they see fit. All of that works, at the conceptual level.<br /> <br /> My issue is that it feels like it remains as a concept.<br /> <br /> The first red flag is the name- I mean, I am sure that the team had reasons to leave the name like that. I guess that they wanted it to ring like &quot;Surrealists&quot; or &quot;Classicists&quot; or something along those lines. You know, make it feel like a art movement- and it works as a artistic movement. Enigmatic Art could totally be a thing- but that returns us to the same problem that we had with CWS. The fact that something works for an artistic movement does not mean it works for a conspiracy- and most artistic movements are not so cohesive to actually create something we could call a &quot;conspiracy&quot;. They rarely have the numbers for it, and most modern and post modern movements are so scattered and fractured and individualistic. In most times the term is used, it is meant to represent a style- I can make a art of a certain style without being belong to a certain movement. Oh, sure, the arts world have a lot of clicks and academies and institutions- that's why it does make a good foundation for a conspiracy. But the name does not reflect some global institution. It reflects an artistic style. And that's a problem because I don't think it works for a conspiracy, as even fractured conspiracies such as the Les Mysteres and the Council of Bones have actual names for them. And names are important both in character and out of character. it is the first impression you get on a group, and when that first impression is &quot;not really a name at all&quot;, well... yeah. Enigmatic Art is a good name for a artistic movement. It is not a good name for a 100 year old occult society. It is barely a name at all.<br /> <br /> Put a pin on that. We are going to return to it later.<br /> <br /> Anyway, let's get back to business. We know that the Enigmatics lack a cohesive history- the writeup suggests that they have been previously a pretty fractured group, with different cells being descendant from different artistic movements, probably as artists had encounters with the darkness which inspired them to create, yet during the last century (probably as globalization and sharing of information became more common), those different groups have started to gather together in order to form an actual conspiracy- or something close enough to it. Again, Hunter has an history of fractured conspiracies, but generally they always have something that binds them together, be it a special social network such as the Chain of the Ascending Ones or an occult force such as spirits for Les Mysteres. We also know that some groups had access to their Endowments before the conspiracy was established- what a conspiracy does is to &quot;mass produce&quot; and &quot;redefine&quot; the chaotic supernatural abilities into a system. That's what separates, let's say, the Lucifuge from the L'Enfant Diabolique, or the exiled witches of Britain from the Knights of St George, or even the mad alchemists of Promethean from, the Ascending Ones. We have, after all, already established in previous posts how Endowments seems to be a process in which a &quot;monster&quot; becomes a &quot;person&quot; within the Code- and that Endowments could, in turn, devolve into Dread Powers when manifested among those who lack that Code, The conspiracy itself claims to be far older than all the artistic styles and movements which currently exist, being some sort of a &quot;primordial art&quot;, which is as old as humanity...<br /> <br /> A primordial art crafted by mad artists which produce cursed works of art through some divine inspiration. Why does that sound so familiar?...<br /> <br /> Mummy: <i>starts crawling out of its grave</i><br /> <br /> No! No! Bad evil corpse! Go back or I'll send a Shulpae upon you!<br /> <br /> Where was I? Ha, yes. The Decieved.<br /> <br /> Like, honestly, it is not that hard to put two and two together to understand that whatever the Enigmatics are playing with is <i>very</i> similar to Seba and the arts of the Deceived. Now, it is not limited for it, obviously- the Divine Fire is another prime candidate for mad inspiration, and one of the example Hunters seems to have created a Pandoran by accident, but the fundamental cursed aspects of Masterworks, and how the key power behind them being the Inspiration which motivated the artist, very much says &quot;yeah, those people are influenced by some starry presence to make them create things that everyone will regret later&quot;. The Enigmatics are the product of many disparate Art Cults, probably sponsored by the Decieved to a degree, or even sprouting spontaneously as the mind of the artist degrades due to them encountering the supernatural (meeting new monsters every time is <i>not</i> good for your mental health by the Code and Integrity, after all). Basically, Inspiration is likely the product of a &quot;Seba seed&quot; lurking in their souls, or at least a cultivation of Sekhem through artistic measures that resonates with the stars above and motivates them to create, to embody their greatest fears and desires within paint and clay and song. Note that this &quot;inspiration through fear&quot; thing which they have going on also strongly resonates with Beast- the question, in that case, is if the Beast resonance is essential or not. I mean, Beast's always has its claws at everyone's businesses, and artists could always have the theme of the collective subconscious and introducing madness in others. Is Inspiration, in that case, actually being derived from the Dark Mother instead of the songs of Seba? Could be, but Inspiration as Sekhem Sorcery requires less steps on the way to achieve a goal- we know that Sekhem Sorcery feeds upon sacrifice (such as mental health), related to arts and creates cursed objects. The dark rituals of the Begotten also demand sacrifice and are also inspired by fear, but the creation of cursed artworks is the missing links. On the other end, the way that meeting monsters specifically still gives some strong Beast vibes. Thankfully, we are in Conspiracy Theory, we can freely mix concepts together as long as they make something interesting in the end.<br /> <br /> So yeah, it seems that Inspiration is more or less solved- a form of Sekhem Sorcery method derived from Deceived rituals and/or Seba manifestations alongside potentially some Kinship like mechanism that unites the different artists through fear of the darkness and makes them drawn to the darkness. Basically, the Inspiration from Fear aspects of Begotten Rites is used as a gate to cultivate Sekhem within the artist by the sacrifice of their own sanity through the Vigil while they make contacts with monsters, which is later manifested through Deceived rituals. Makes sense, as much as anything makes sense in this thread. I think that the inclusion of Beast cosmology in here is important because it helps us solve why those artists who leave the conspiracy lose their Inspiration, even though they remain scarred by it- that is, if the Primordial Dream is the gate for Inspiration, the artist must have their ideals resonating with aspects of the Dream in order to be inspired by it. To turn your back against the conspiracy means, in essence, to deny the ideals that you seek to manifest in your art- the Enigmatics, again, are an idea, a style, a concept. By abandoning the artistic style the defines the conspiracy, you lose access to the primordial Fear which inspires you, which in turn denies you the access required to cultivate Sekhem. The use of Mummy cosmology, in turn, helps us answer another, just as important question about the Engimatics- why they don't actually have a name?<br /> <br /> Because they are Nameless.<br /> <br /> See, I told you we will return to this part.<br /> <br /> Being Nameless is a fundamental part of being immortal- steal a subject's name, and it becomes a object in your hands. Steal your own name, and no one can hold power upon you. by denying themselves a name, the Enigmatics can not be targeted by those who seek to disrupt their work- such as the Arisen and the Judges, and even the Decieved, the masters of True Names. The greatest enigma about the conspiracy is that they have no name of their own, allowing their artists to protect themselves from the jealous, angry, hungry deities which roam the Twilight or hunger below the earth. Still, I would personally, call the conspiracy <b>Enigma</b> instead of the Enigmatics- a name which is not a name, hidden from the world by not being described into words and living merely as Inspiration. heck, what if the artistic style itself <i>is</i> the seba which powers the Inspiration of the conspiracy? And that by keeping it nameless, it can not be tracked an harvested by the Deceived. This could also offer a-Beast independent mechanism for how Inspiration remains exclusive in the hands of the conspiracy (as Fate probably decrees who gains access to this primordial Seba in the first place), but I still can't ignore the itch that there is some Dark Mother aspects to this whole process. Again, Inspiration through Fear is a too strong theme to ignore.<br /> <br /> Anyway, with or without Beast, we have probably solved both Inspiration and how the organization forms and operates (again, Fate likely to have chosen which artists to get Inspired and slowly tied them together- perhaps as a means for the original &quot;Enigma Seba&quot; to fully manifest in reality). However, there is still one missing link in here, which is my main criticism about the conspiracy. Their Vigil.<br /> <br /> Look, I get it that members of the conspiracy <i>can</i> hunt down monsters in a violent manner. I get it that some of them probably do so. The text even suggests how those who do it may do it. <i>However</i>, the very first sentence in the Enemy section is &quot;<i>Enigmatics don\u2019t focus on slaying monsters \u2014 or even on seeking new materials for their art. Most are content encountering the supernatural</i>&quot;. Can the Enigmatics go around and use their Masterworks to hunt down monsters? Yes, obviously. But <i>the text claims it is not the default</i>. Their <i>default</i> Vigil is &quot;go around, meet new monsters and places, get inspired, go back and make some Endowments&quot;. That is, <i>their Endowments are a product of the Vigil, not assistance in it</i>. This is, to me, a very big Red Flag as I firmly believe that Endowments should be tools of the Vigil- they are methods to uphold it. Yet if the Vigil of the Enigmatics is &quot;getting inspired&quot;, and Masterowrks are the result of &quot;getting inspired&quot;, they are not directly in assistance for <i>their own Vigil</i>. Sure, you could say &quot;oh the Vigil is dangerous, they are using those Endowments in order to protect themselves from monsters or control their targets so they could be safe while getting inspired by them&quot;. To which I ask &quot;yes, but <i>is it the prime objective of those Endowments?</i>&quot;, because it seems to me that those Endowments are a product of the Vigil, the result of hunting down a monster. Also- <i>Important Red Flag</i>- the artists does NOT controls the outcome of the Masterwork. Sure, the <i>player</i> does, but not the <i>artist</i>. We know it because if they succeed at creating a Masterwork, they gain <i>insight</i> to the Masterwork's <i>agenda</i>. This is stated directly in the description text- those objects <i>have an agenda</i>, and it is an <i>evil agenda</i>. Enigma is not under any control over its own action- they are tools at the hand of this primordial scale Seba or Fate or the Dark Mother or the Nameless Gods know what in order to <i>give birth to cursed objects</i> while having <i>no control over their own actions</i>. Something inspires them, causes them to chase the supernatural and create cursed artworks that will harm innocent lives. We know that they have no actual choice in the subject because (a) we know that Inspiration is spontaneous and (b) we know that they are Conditioned with both Inspired and Obsession. <i>They don't actually have a choice in creating</i>. They are not really in choice for pursuing the vigil- they can leave, sure, <i>but they are still Obsessed</i>. Their are slaves to their own creations, to their own inspirations, to the Vigil and the dark forces who abuse it. They <i>must</i> hunt, they <i>must</i> create.<br /> <br /> But they do have one choice.<br /> <br /> What to do with their own cursed creations.<br /> <br /> Look at what we have established- that Masterworks are dangerous. They cause people to get addicted to their own Vices, to encourage cannibalism, turn people into stalkers, steal their memories, demand human sacrifices. They have a will of their own- a cursed will, and they seek to corrupt the world. This is why I think that the true Vigil that Enigma faces against is not towards the monsters- it is towards their own creations. They have to create them- but they can contain them, or use them in ways which are beneficial. Some may use them in ways which contain monsters. Others may seek to entomb them, hide them from the world- or present them only in special, places where they do not cause much harm. Or some may actually want to put them in the open, expose the world to the supernatural- but I assume that they re the minority, and probably the ones who quickly degenerate into madness. Releasing a song of cannibalism to the world or allowing statues to claim human sacrifices is probably <i>not</i> very healthy for your mental health, and those who let their creations roam free will quickly fall under their influence and become slashers or cultists. As a result, I imagine that most of their Vigil is about making sure that their own cursed creations are left satisfied and contained, or, as the writer have said &quot;play with fire&quot; when they decide to use them to actually hunt monsters (I would say &quot;playing with a lava stream&quot; but whatever floats your boat). Their goal is to keep the Masterworks in a place where they won't harm innocents- and stop greedy fingers, such as the AKD or the Deathless, from abusing them.<br /> <br /> Ok, so now we know how the conspiracy works, why they have the name they have (or lack of), what are their Endowments and what is their true Vigil. In that case, what we are left with? Ah yes- their agenda.<br /> <br /> Their &quot;cursed agenda&quot;.<br /> <br /> Look, it is very clear that in a vacuum, Engima has no agenda of its own- the Enigmatics create because some external, supernatural force drives them to create. As such, the true agenda of the conspiracy is to not merely satisfy the curses of its masterworks- it is to satisfy the curse of the Inspiration itself. If there is indeed a cosmic level Seba manipulating this whole operation through the stars and Fate, its goal is to manifest- and if the Seba is indeed the artistic style itself, it drives humanity as a collective to perfect the Enigmatic Art style and manifest accordingly. Let is sink for a moment- we are talking about a Seba which takes the form of an <i>artistic style</i>. This means that the power of this Seba would be reality bending- and the curse it would produce would be just as terrifying. This will be the end of the world as we know it. In that case, what would Engima do about it?<br /> <br /> Find a way to contain it.<br /> <br /> Engima are both the hands which would bring the apocalypse and the only ones who may stop it. Them starting to organize in the recent years? It is not merely to share ideas and create more Masterworks- it is to create a system that will bind and contain the Seba, keep it both protected from the Deceived who may try to abuse it and the world from its formation. We know about Neithian Architecture, after all- the use of relics in order to manipulate Sekhem? Well, what if Masterworks are not merely contained in random place? What if they are being distributed in a manner that would create a world-wide Lifeweb of seba in a way that, when the Seba would be finally manifested, it would be trapped, contained, and kept under control. To stop the threat that it would pose upon the world, and cleanse the Art from its own curses. Heck, what if their plan is to truly cure Sekhem, the lifeforce of the world, from curses all together? No more mad statues and paintings which trap souls within them. Seba are, after all, eternal- being drawn from the stars, far from Ammut's hungry maw. Contain the Seba, bind it to Earth, cleanse Sekhem from the touch of the Devourer and make art pure once more. Make the world eternal, free from entropy. Steal the world's true name and make it into an enigma. Make the world nameless.<br /> <br /> And you thought that crafting masterworks is playing with fire.<br /> <br /> ************************************************** ************************************************** ****************<br /> <br /> Aaaand done. Again, with all of the criticism that I have towards the Enigmatics, this is a version that not only I can live with, but actually like. I really think that shifting their Vigil from monsters to their own creations is the correct way to go, but to each person their own. Amway, next will be Operation Nebula- so stay tuned, and keep your tinfoil hats on! Until next time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-20T15:52:16-07:00","id":1515698,"title":"","content":"I'll admit i was worried about this one, but I like what you did. It feels faithful to the write-up, with a nice twist on the role of endowments.<br /> <br /> For the role of the dark mother, is it possible to claim that Masterworks may house astral reflections of monsters (or even some kind of larval horror), thus explaining how these statues, which represent monstrosity/the supernatural, gain an agenda that seems to broadly fit what the artist thought at the moment of inspiration?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T13:47:19-07:00","id":1515781,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Primordial newcomer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515698#post1515698\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll admit i was worried about this one, but I like what you did. It feels faithful to the write-up, with a nice twist on the role of endowments.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is not my goal to make any heavy rewrites to any of those conspiracies, even if there are points which I would have done some things very different. The main reason is that I am mentally incapable of ignoring canon- which would have made things so much easier if I could, for example, ignore the honestly bad line about the CoB killing members who use their Endowments in public or for personal gain. The end result is my trying to reconcile the things which I don't think make sense in the writeup in a way which would fit how I could use the conspiracy (hence why I call it &quot;rebuilding&quot; instead of &quot;rewriting&quot;)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T13:51:23-07:00","id":1515782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515781#post1515781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is not my goal to make any heavy rewrites to any of those conspiracies, even if there are points which I would have done some things very different. The main reason is that I am mentally incapable of ignoring canon- which would have made things so much easier if I could, for example, ignore the honestly bad line about the CoB killing members who use their Endowments in public or for personal gain. The end result is my trying to reconcile the things which I don't think make sense in the writeup in a way which would fit how I could use the conspiracy (hence why I call it &quot;rebuilding&quot; instead of &quot;rewriting&quot;)</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's mostly more &quot;recontextualization&quot; than &quot;rebuilding&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T14:38:24-07:00","id":1515788,"title":"","content":"I blame my Debate club activity for the use of the word, then. We use &quot;rebuilding&quot; in a probably wider context which includes recontextualization","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T22:09:38-07:00","id":1515822,"title":"","content":"Might I just say that I\u2019m really enjoying this thread. Thank you for this, LostLight.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-22T08:39:44-07:00","id":1515868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>White Oak Dragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515822#post1515822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Might I just say that I\u2019m really enjoying this thread. Thank you for this, LostLight.</div> </div> </div> </div>Happy to hear you enjoy those bits! As I said, I really like the new book and it has a lot of good stuff, this thread is just me finding ways to connect to the new groups you provided in the companion <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-22T09:22:40-07:00","id":1515873,"title":"","content":"Honestly would love a compacts and conspiracies style fan supplement from you Light. Would enjoy what you would write for the Keepers of the Source pitch<br /> <br /> also, perhaps the Enigmatics are called that because they also represent a question of just WHAT is it the supernatural inspire in them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-22T14:00:40-07:00","id":1515892,"title":"","content":"It could be, even though it is likely to meant to represent the fact that they are dealing with the unknown. I still like my theory about it being intentionally nameless, however :P<br /> <br /> Anyway, moving to the next conspiracy-<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Operation: Nebula</b></span><br /> <br /> Mo older brother is an Ancient Astronaut Theorist. He doesn't just believe in aliens, or watch the History channel unironically, or even &quot;just&quot; believe that the aliens were the ones to build the pyramids or Stonehenge. He has a computer which is detached from the internet on which he saves all of the data he has about aliens and the research he does about them, including his evidence, because he claims that Google intentionally censures information about aliens. When I started my bachelor degree, his words to me were &quot;don't forget to always seek after the truth- don't let those guys in the academy tell you what is truth and what isn't. Don't let them lie to you&quot;. He is also a very conservative person which makes conversations with him very difficult, but that generally works together. Also, he totally believes in a left winged LGBTQ+ conspiracy which stops democracy and destroy our values and all of those nice things.<br /> <br /> In short, it is hard to live with a conspiracy theorists. <br /> <br /> I mean, I am sure that not all Ancient Astronaut Theorists are radical conservative fellows- but there is a reason that it is a common trait. The idea that the institution tries to control you, hide the truth from you, use all kinds of substances and experiments on you without your consent. My other sibling is also a conspiracy theorist, even though he goes much more to the religious aspects of it, letting religious figures tell him about the chips in COVID vaccines and all of that nice things. What I am trying to say that it could be easy to poke fun and such people- but those are really frustrating personally traits to deal with, and which means that I have too much knowledge about how such people think, and as such I probably understand too well how the average ON argent think- that the truth is out there, that the institute hides it, and that insidious entities use it to control our society. And the reason those people may have the tendency to become very conservative is because you can just substitute &quot;aliens&quot; with some minority group that you hate or some &quot;pure race&quot; theory and you get to the same result. Still, regardless of the political opinions that conspiracy theorists may hold, they generally have a common trait- you can't trust institutes. They are all corrupted, trying to hide the truth, and seek to control your life.<br /> <br /> And in the context of Hunter- they do have a point. But in that case, the question I had after first hearing about ON was &quot;how can they be a part of a conspiracy, when it represents everything that they are against?&quot;<br /> <br /> The actual writeup actually found a good way to answer that question- they don't understand that they are a conspiracy.<br /> <br /> ON is a yet another very young conspiracy. Considering that the organization have started through online communities, they have probably being formed during the late 90's at best, and most likely in the early 2000's. So like CWS, ON have around 20 years of existence, formed when someone discovered that what he believed to be a conspiracy theory was, in fact, real- or at least, part of it. In order to find the truth, he started communicating online with other conspiracy theorists, creating a society dedicated for discovering the secrets of aliens and their agenda, as well as developing methods to extract special &quot;alien cores&quot; from either alien technology or corpses, later being used to develop the so called Xenotechnology. Here we already see a number of red flags, which become even clearer when we look at the Endowments section- as only members of ON are able to extract alien cores, that the gizmos all look very sci-fi in appearance, and that in the hands of people who aren't part of the conspiracy, the gizmos start to fail and degrade. That makes it sound almost like a... dream? Fantasy? Shared hallucination? This is better reinforced by the fact that there isn't any description of what &quot;aliens&quot; are- which is in a strong contrast to Lucifuge's definition of &quot;demons&quot;. There is not &quot;Infernal Dread Power&quot; which distinguish a alien from a non-alien. All monsters are aliens. At least- all monsters that the hunter believes that they are.<br /> <br /> On the surface, the jump between online forums to conspiracy to Endowments seems like a very huge leap- but by taking into account how their Endowments work, things start falling in place. The members of ON all have a conspiracy theory- they truly believe that aliens are real, and that they control the world. The funny thing is, that in the CofD, they are not <i>wrong</i>. The God Machine could very well be an alien computer seeded upon Earth. Planetary Angels invade Earth's Shadow, while Void Leviathans are draw to the life it offers and then try to crush into its depths. The Gentry kidnap and mutilate people in a manner which is very similar to aliens. The Visitors outright claim that they are a part of an interstellar struggle between different alien factions, while hosting the brains of their vessels. Fears of the Other often manifest themselves as alien entities, allowing those Begotten to take their form. Heck, even Cheiron are potentially controlled by aliens. There are alien-like entities everywhere in the CofD- <i>and they are all equivalently irrelevant</i>. Why? Because when you can extract a vampire core to power your stealth cloak, either you need to admit that vampires are also aliens, or that this is whole mess simply does not make sense. Like, sure, Marcel Foster's dead apparently have been experimented upon by <i>something</i>, and <i>someone</i> did stole the journal- but is that related to the aliens described by the conspiracy? There are so many different conspiracies in the CofD- either the aliens control them all, or that the conspiracy is much more complicated than that.<br /> <br /> So basically what we have is that Xenotechnology works a lot more like a shared hallucination than some actual technology- but counterpoint: Xenotechnology is real. The alien cores are physical entities that can be interacted with. The gizmos are objects that have physical, direct effects. In this case, let's give you a counter-counter point- Xenotechnology works <i>a lot</i> more like an act of sorcery than as an item Endowments. It has a certain amount of Successes needed in order to produce a certain object, with Xenotechnology being a 1-5 Endowment which provides schematics (rituals?) in order to produce gizmos. It even has a cost- not in the form of Willpower, but in the form of the alien core. Counter-counter-counterpoint- the Clash of Wills for gizmos does not use Occult, unlike the rest of the Endowments. Instead, it use Crafts. That leans to the idea that there isn't much &quot;magic&quot; in the creation process, but that the act is technological, to a degree. It depends on the skills of the hunter in understanding how technology and engineering work, not in how to manipulate the occult energies which are embedded in the very fabric of the universe. As such, there is a technological side to Xenotechnology. Counter-counter-counter-counterpoint, artifice is also a medium for magic, and if Xenotechnology is relying on some shared hallucination, it should depend on the ability to perceive the object as real, and how well the hunter can define it as an actual technology. The strength of the gizmo depends on how well the hunter understands technology, because the better they understand it, the more &quot;real&quot; the gizmo becomes. A conspiracy theorist could make a gizmo that is based on half baked theories, but reality is quick to deny it. One who has a degree in engineering, however, can make a much more convincing technology instead.<br /> <br /> Ok, so Xenotechnology is some short of a shared hallucination that can be crafted into form by those who take part in it and subscribe to its theories, basically a form of technomagic. In a way, it works a lot like the Technocracy from M:tAs, yet more wacky. However, magic does not come out of nowhere- something have created that shared hallucination. Something created the conspiracy. People don't just develop the ability to extract alien cores and build special equipment out of discussing it with others online. Something organized the conspiracy to a degree it is functional, without the conspiracy theories actually believing that they are a part of a shadowy organization. This is the problem with online communities- you can never know who is on the other side of the screen. Is LostLight an actual person? Or am I just a highly advanced AI produced by the aliens who control the government? Obviously you assume that I am a real person with too much free time (I actually don't have that much free time, and yes it means that the little bit that I have I use to write wacky theories about a RPG, but nevermind that). However, if I was an advance alien AI, could you have noticed it? Or if, perhaps, I was an intelligent ape? Three dwarves in a trench coat? Am I vegetarian? Vegan? Am I trans? Gay? Bi? Am I conservative or leftist? Is my brother even exist, or did I just made him up just to make me feel relatable, or give me some sense of expertise about the subject? Am I, LostLight, even real?<br /> <br /> The internet is a shared hallucinations, my friends. We are all part of the conspiracy.<br /> <br /> Ok, going back from the highly philosophical argument (too much Philosophy Tube), the point that was made is that under the cover anonymity, no one can actually who they are talking to online- and it is really unclear how ON actually makes sure that &quot;aliens&quot; do not infiltrate their networks. Probably they are doing some security measures, but considering that most monsters who do use the internet know well enough how to act &quot;human&quot;, it is likely that they won't really be able to stop them. However, here comes the actual defense mechanism of the conspiracy- <i>they are crackpots</i>. Monsters will <i>not</i> take them seriously. Oh, sure, there is likely a Guardian or an Invictus member who spend a few hours a day rolling through their feeds in the case that the conspiracy has managed to somehow uncover something they shouldn't, but the amount of misdirection that the conspiracy leads itself to believe in, and the fact that no one will take them seriously because of their Ancient Astronaut theories means that 99% of the time, they don't actually need to do anything. Even if, let's say, they manage to uncover some instance of them meeting with a vampire, they are likely to claim it was some sort of a &quot;parasite alien&quot;, and when they'll go around telling people about &quot;parasite aliens&quot;, no one will believe in them. Because they are crackpots. Because they make no sense. They are a living &quot;masquerade breach&quot;, yet they are also a living masquerade by themselves. They are a walking, living paradox- people who know the truth, yet their truth is denied by the world, for it is not true, even though it is.<br /> <br /> Wait- isn't there a whole gameline about people who &quot;know the truth&quot; that the rest of the world denies because it lives in a lie, and that there are some ancient entities which control the population in an attempt to stop the truth from being spread, and that if they'll try to spread the truth to others people will deny it because it is true and basically leave us with a living paradox?<br /> <br /> Mage: <i>knocks on the door</i> &quot;May I come it?&quot;<br /> <br /> Me: &quot;I promise I'll be back in, like 5 minutes. Just let me finish the companion conspiracies and we are back to making up new Orders and such&quot;<br /> <br /> Mage: &quot;You promise?&quot;<br /> <br /> Me: &quot;Yes, yes- trust me. Just the companion... <span style=\"font-size:10px\">and maybe a similar thread about compacts</span>&quot;<br /> <br /> Mage: &quot;What?&quot;<br /> <br /> Me: &quot;What? I didn't say anything. Move along, move along&quot;<br /> <br /> Where was I? Ah, right.<br /> <br /> Operation Nebula is controlled by an Abyssal Intruder.<br /> <br /> (I can almost hear Arc yelling at me &quot;those are the Gentry! Those are obviously the Gentry! Or at least the God Machine!&quot;. Alas, almost)<br /> <br /> I mean- think about it. Something allows those people to create impossible technology by manifesting alien cores. Sure, the God Machine could be behind of it. The Gentry could be behind of it. The Onachus could be behind of it. The Contagion may even be behind of that- the shared hallucination spreading like a plague and causing people to make reality bleed and produce alien technology from their subconscious. But there is something with the Abyss that fits- the Abyss has been known to infest souls and replace the truths of the world with products of its own. It is known to be drawn to those with a less stable mind and seep through the cracks. It is known to be related to the world of dreams. It is known to be related to madness and denial of reality. The Abyss, the dark ocean behind the world, is also often called the Void- and alien entities do creep into reality from it. The Abyss works narratively as a metaphor to the Great Beyond and eldritch gods, while also working thematically to explain Xenotechnology. In that case, however, we need to understand how this Intruder looks like, and how it operates- after all, all Intruders are based around replacing aspects of reality with themselves- and in that case, how the Intruder which is behind this whole mess actually operates?<br /> <br /> Probably, by the internet.<br /> <br /> I mean, Intruders generally leak into the world by accident, when strange patterns are arranged in strange ways- and nothing is better at producing such patterns than the internet. Random combinations of words, numbers and topics, openly discussed transgressions and sharing of radical ideas. All of that serving as a rich ground for the Abyss to open up in the wrong place and the wrong time. It could be that this Intruder has started small- substituting some parts of the internet, creating a literal &quot;dark web&quot;. Creating false personas that mimic humans, bots made out of eldritch terror, propagating misinformation in an attempt to hide the truth, or conspiracy theories in order to lure agents in. Some of those conspiracy theories are more radical than the others, but all are meant to propagate and invest radical narratives which would open up those who believe in them to the Abyss- basically, taking the &quot;dead internet&quot; theory and putting it on steroids. And as this alien internet grows through the internet, it is met with a bunch of conspiracy theories who try to give context to the madness of the CofD. It mimics their opinions and reflects them- yes, you are correct. There are aliens everywhere. They control the government, the media, the pharmaceutical companies. They have controlled humanity since its inception. Yes, there are aliens. Yes, they have advanced technology, you are absolutely right. Yes, you can harvest that technology. Yes, you can make advanced weapons out of it. You want proof? Very well. Why don't you try to harvest one? Oh, you found this object inside? What if you will build something out of it. I found this schematics- it is completely true. Believe in it. Believe, and it will be true. <br /> <br /> And they believed. And it became true.<br /> <br /> So yeah, it would be very, very tempting to call this Intruder the Echo Chamber- and I think I will use this term, as it is fitting. The Echo Chamber's strength is by the fact that it can take misinformation and make it real, disrupting reality in the process. Its weakness is that it is not independent- it must mimic the thoughts of others, which means that those affected by it have a lot of power and control. The Echo Chamber is only passive, and the more people are engaged with it, the more it is able to allow them to manifest their fantasies into reality. In that case, you may ask, what is actually the endgame of the Echo Chamber? What it actually plans on doing? The answer i very simple- to simply satisfy the wills of those engaged with it. Those conspiracy theorists want aliens? Then they are getting aliens.<br /> <br /> An whole invasion of aliens.<br /> <br /> That's the peak of the shared hallucination that ON have- the creation of a full blown alien invasion. Spaceships, tripodes, lizardmen, the grey- everything you want, all at once. Conspiracy theories will become true- an alien base will be uncovered in Antarctica, Earth is hollow and full of dinosaurs, whatever you can think about- it is real now. The truth will be revealed to the world- no longer they will be laughed upon as crackpots. No longer their truth will be denied. The invasion will come- and they will be there to stop it. Or, perhaps, to join it. Depending on who is more popular on their social media. After all, the Echo Chamber will reward those who are the most engaged within its dark platforms.<br /> <br /> So don't forget to like and subscribe, everyone! Who knows? Maybe it is your only way to stop the aliens from coming to you.<br /> <br /> ******************************************<br /> <br /> If you ever wanted to know- no, I do not plan those posts in advance. I just start writing and go with the flow. All of this existential discussing and criticism about internet culture? Yeah I also have no idea from where it came from.<br /> <br /> Anyway- hope you liked it! There were seriously many ways I could have run with it. I considered the God Machine, some Gentry manipulation, or even have the Visitors as actual aliens that manipulate the conspiracy from beneath the tinfoil hats of their members and that the alien cores are some form of concentrated Sekhem or something along those lines, or even just some Astral bs, so let me know what do you think and which other candidates you would have used to explain their Endowments. Anyway, next will be the Survivors Club- both among my favorite groups and one which I think needs some recontextualization in order to close what I think are some of the logical gaps in the writeup. Anyway, see you next time!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-22T16:33:46-07:00","id":1515908,"title":"","content":"I was very excited for this one and you delivered. The Intruder patron was VERY NICE. I may play around with gentry manipulation still (because conspiracy theories, especially ancient astronaught theory, requires quite a sense of wonder), but damn the echo chamber sounds great even without a conspiracy.<br /> <br /> Looking forward to the Survivors Club. Probably the best Endowment in the book, and I'm curious to see how you do concerning their organization (since I believe this makes them the only conspiracy besides the Lucifuge to have an innate Endowment, correct?) Also, lets remember Charmers/Hypnos have a vested interest to make organizations now according to 2nd edition","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-22T17:46:19-07:00","id":1515916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515892#post1515892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> (I can almost hear Arc yelling at me &quot;those are the Gentry! Those are obviously the Gentry! Or at least the God Machine!&quot;. Alas, almost)</div> </div> </div> </div>Bruh, why you gotta call me out like that? Though I'll note you literally could have lifted the &quot;compromised by monsters&quot; entry mentioned in the TtF thread, being &quot;Probably Half-Directed by Either a Changeling or a Demon or a Demon in a Changeling's Cover (Operation Nebula)&quot;<br /> <br /> I still have an Ascending Ones rebuttal I gotta get out that's more important than this, but I will confess this one strikes me as one of your wildest ones out there, and one that gets me yelling Occam's Razor. I think you got a little too hung up on the conspiracy theorist angle of it all and the overall relationship of conspiracy to truth and data and the consequences of a post-truth world-which, hey, makes for fun theories and gets into grousing about the fact we are dealing with a post-truth era and how that sucks, but it does feel like reaching beyond what you need to grasp.<br /> <br /> So, tackling points:<br /> <br /> 1)&quot;How can they be a part of a conspiracy, when it represents everything that they are against?&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't think this is as much of a trip up as you think it is, particularly since we're this side of the January 6th Insurrection. Conspiracy theorist's problems with conspiracies tend to not actually be about the existence of such so much as what they think they're doing-they're quite happy to imagine themselves as part of a conspiracy if it's counteracting those malevolent ones. QAnon, for whatever else can be said about it, is a very illustrative example, as it's directly establishing it's adherents into a militarizable conspiracy even as it lays out the conspiracy acting against them. It's about being on the wrong side of power, and I imagine many people are just as happy with playing as dirty as the opposition as to have a dirty opposition in the first place.<br /> <br /> Anyways, I feel like the bit where Foster makes connections and unifies them under the codename Nebula is sufficent enough to validate that, yes, ON agents know they're in a conspiracy.<br /> <br /> 2) The Cores and the Aliens Themselves<br /> <br /> I feel like the reading that ON can draw cores where no one else can, and how they can draw it from any kind of monster, is another one of those things that run right past where the assumptions start, including a tangential on how every monster has a heart, so there's your core right there-but we're running ahead of ourselves.<br /> <br /> ON's interests are in aliens, and while on this side of the fog of war it can be hard to discern the distinguish the difference between &quot;monster&quot; and &quot;alien&quot;, the write-up is allusive enough about a distinction that I think it's fair to say ON has lines between the Aliens, who are engineering the invasion and domination of the world, and the monsters they create in that pursuit. This is doubly supported by the fact that while the appelation of alien can apply to a great deal of monsters, some games are definitely more alien-y than others-in particular, Changeling, Demon, and Deviant all more readily deal with the themes and tropes of alien stories more than others, starting from alien abduction and ending on governmental conspiracies regarding the manipulation of such. This is supported by a lot of the text having allusions towards those first two games, with the high-tech facilities folded in space pretty readily sounding like Descent's Facilities, and the mention of the thorny homeworld in the Cyberglass endowment.<br /> <br /> Part of what makes that particularly compelling is the fact that fairies and demons (and deviants) are among the splats more likely to have something on them that is likely to be &quot;core-like&quot;-a Mannikin's hearts, the Summer Courtier's Sword Manifestation, the Processes of any given demon, the Coactive growths or Invasive's prosthetics. These are all things that feels toyable.<br /> <br /> Obviously I don't to leave it at that, since part of the fun of having the target be aliens is that it allows for a breadth of experience more specific enemies would deny (in particular, it keeps the Promtheans on the table, and if you think I'm gonna do my Stargate peeps dirty by suggesting there's no way ON would go after the Arisen, you are wrong), but again, core experiences for definition and all that.<br /> <br /> 3) Xenotechnology<br /> <br /> THe simplest way to go at this one is the question of &quot;mass shared hallucination&quot; versus &quot;arcane physics&quot;, which one seems more likely in the more objectively-driven Chronicles, and in particular if we accept that ON's operations are intended to be a lot more confined in terms of targets than every monster out there. The biggest argument lies in if we're also engaging with the fairy side of things, how does that work. The obvious answer of &quot;like magnets&quot; asides, the hallucination appraoch would be more approachable if it had a model more akin towards spellcasting factors in Mage or Realms in Changeling the Dreaming, but that it makes the case that schematics are more fixated, and are shareable for any of their actual mutability strikes me that it hews away from that model (Masterworks are more the crafting thing that leans into mass shared hallucination).<br /> <br /> Also, I don't wanna bother with this in talking about ON on the other thread but it's admittedly only tangential here, but whatever: Xenotechnology continues the illusion of a march of things that continues to make Genius less and less relevant, and I am somewhere between very okay and very meh with that.<br /> <br /> 4) Roy Elliot<br /> <br /> For whatever else can be said about the overall wibbliness of the conspiracy in terms of of what's going on under the hood for it, one thing stands out pretty strongly-Roy Elliot is a very convienent actor. Foster spends all these years running from pursuants and never getting a clean lead, and then when he meets Elliot, who's as dubious as Marcel's father, all of a sudden the man has compelling enough evidence of an alien invasion with multiple competitors and several high tech facilities that Foster then commits to forging Nebula? That, as the kids like to say, is sus-and more than that, it's shockingly coherent for being so convenient, and it doesn't carry the stink of nihilism that usually surrounds the presence of the Abyss.<br /> <br /> And I admit, I have run out of steam, so I'll have to get back to this point later. <hr />But to be fair to the theory, let's square the obvious with the theory-the God-Machine and it's shit <i>does</i> have more in common with the Abyss than any other peer in the conventional monstersphere as both have this element of contradictory invader from outside of reality, so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T06:20:29-07:00","id":1515942,"title":"","content":"I haven't gotten <i>Tending the Flame</i> yet, so all of this is my introduction to these Conspiracies. I wonder if the original lives up to this thread...<br /> <br /> Anyways, if Xenotechnology truly is created through belief, it will carry some very disturbing ramifications. And I mean that in the more immediate sense than an alien base manifasting in Antarctica. The question to ask is: what is a valid target for extraction? Could an operative hunt down a dog with severe mange, convinced they caught the Chupacabra, and successfully extract a Core from it? If not, what if that same dog was Ghouled by a vampire? What if it was merely controlled through Animalism? What if it was a person controlled through Dominate? What if it was a person with no connection to the supernatural whatsoever? It only takes one act of violent paranoia to kill an innocent person, and have Xenotechnology produce proof of your righteousness. Proof that can be shared with others. And if that person belongs to a group you disagree with, very soon local ON may become the biggest threat to mundane pople, dwarfing even monsters in the shadows.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T13:18:45-07:00","id":1515961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515916#post1515916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Bruh, why you gotta call me out like that? Though I'll note you literally could have lifted the &quot;compromised by monsters&quot; entry mentioned in the TtF thread, being &quot;Probably Half-Directed by Either a Changeling or a Demon or a Demon in a Changeling's Cover (Operation Nebula)&quot;<br /> <br /> I still have an Ascending Ones rebuttal I gotta get out that's more important than this, but I will confess this one strikes me as one of your wildest ones out there, and one that gets me yelling Occam's Razor. I think you got a little too hung up on the conspiracy theorist angle of it all and the overall relationship of conspiracy to truth and data and the consequences of a post-truth world-which, hey, makes for fun theories and gets into grousing about the fact we are dealing with a post-truth era and how that sucks, but it does feel like reaching beyond what you need to grasp.</div> </div> </div> </div>It could be that I left my Occam's Razor at my lab :P<br /> <br /> But seriously- my first instinct was to use either Changeling or Demon (or the Visitors from Immortals) as a base for ON. As I mentioned, I do not plan those things in advance- I just start writing and see where the train goes. The addition of Echo Chamber honestly felt to me like reaching- but I really felt that having Xenotechnology as a some of sorcery based shared hallucination was appropriate, and all that while I've had Division 6 rumbling in the back of my head and the whole aesthetics of the Abyss as a great, black void filled with alien horrors. Even if we are using the shared hallucination as a base, the Abyss is probably not the immediate candidate for it- but again, this is how things went in this post. Having the source of it being, let's ay, a exiled True Fae or something like that should work just fine- but I still feel like it is the most thematic explanation, even if not the simplest one.<br /> <br /> I still like Echo Chamber as a concept. It's definitely gonna show up in my game, with or without ON.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think this is as much of a trip up as you think it is, particularly since we're this side of the January 6th Insurrection. Conspiracy theorist's problems with conspiracies tend to not actually be about the existence of such so much as what they think they're doing-they're quite happy to imagine themselves as part of a conspiracy if it's counteracting those malevolent ones. QAnon, for whatever else can be said about it, is a very illustrative example, as it's directly establishing it's adherents into a militarizable conspiracy even as it lays out the conspiracy acting against them. It's about being on the wrong side of power, and I imagine many people are just as happy with playing as dirty as the opposition as to have a dirty opposition in the first place.<br /> <br /> Anyways, I feel like the bit where Foster makes connections and unifies them under the codename Nebula is sufficent enough to validate that, yes, ON agents know they're in a conspiracy. </div> </div> </div>If it wasn't clear, I do think that the writeup did a good job in explaining this part. I personally think that QAnon, for example, also don't really understand what they are- some do, probably, but did the people who got on the Capitol actually understood that they were manipulated? I think they didn't, but on the other hand I am not American. My brothers are a good example for it- they say that &quot;the media forces progressive agenda&quot; upon them, and that they &quot;see reality clearly&quot;, but that's because they themselves don't understand that some people with power and money made sure to construct the conspiracy theories they believe in. Again, it is a subject I had the &quot;delightful experience&quot; to live with such people, which obviously colours how I perceive this conspiracy, but I guess that's just part of being a human or something like that. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I feel like the reading that ON can draw cores where no one else can, and how they can draw it from any kind of monster, is another one of those things that run right past where the assumptions start, including a tangential on how every monster has a heart, so there's your core right there-but we're running ahead of ourselves.<br /> <br /> ON's interests are in aliens, and while on this side of the fog of war it can be hard to discern the distinguish the difference between &quot;monster&quot; and &quot;alien&quot;, the write-up is allusive enough about a distinction that I think it's fair to say ON has lines between the Aliens, who are engineering the invasion and domination of the world, and the monsters they create in that pursuit. This is doubly supported by the fact that while the appelation of alien can apply to a great deal of monsters, some games are definitely more alien-y than others-in particular, Changeling, Demon, and Deviant all more readily deal with the themes and tropes of alien stories more than others, starting from alien abduction and ending on governmental conspiracies regarding the manipulation of such. This is supported by a lot of the text having allusions towards those first two games, with the high-tech facilities folded in space pretty readily sounding like Descent's Facilities, and the mention of the thorny homeworld in the Cyberglass endowment.<br /> <br /> Part of what makes that particularly compelling is the fact that fairies and demons (and deviants) are among the splats more likely to have something on them that is likely to be &quot;core-like&quot;-a Mannikin's hearts, the Summer Courtier's Sword Manifestation, the Processes of any given demon, the Coactive growths or Invasive's prosthetics. These are all things that feels toyable.<br /> <br /> Obviously I don't to leave it at that, since part of the fun of having the target be aliens is that it allows for a breadth of experience more specific enemies would deny (in particular, it keeps the Promtheans on the table, and if you think I'm gonna do my Stargate peeps dirty by suggesting there's no way ON would go after the Arisen, you are wrong), but again, core experiences for definition and all that. </div> </div> </div>In the Discord, the writer have stated that in their intentions, ON treat all monsters as aliens, so while you could describe it as &quot;only certain monsters have cores&quot;, I think that assumption is &quot;all monsters have cores&quot;. It also seems that I misread that ON agents lose the ability to extract cores from &quot;aliens&quot; without the conspiracy, even though the text does seem to suggest it (namely, them extracting an extraterrestrial fuel cell out of a cryptid. Sure, you could say that they just know where to look, but... I don't know, sounds fishy). Even if they can still find cores out of nowhere, the fact that gizmos break apart after one scene still makes it suspicious- but that already leads to your last point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> THe simplest way to go at this one is the question of &quot;mass shared hallucination&quot; versus &quot;arcane physics&quot;, which one seems more likely in the more objectively-driven Chronicles, and in particular if we accept that ON's operations are intended to be a lot more confined in terms of targets than every monster out there. The biggest argument lies in if we're also engaging with the fairy side of things, how does that work. The obvious answer of &quot;like magnets&quot; asides, the hallucination appraoch would be more approachable if it had a model more akin towards spellcasting factors in Mage or Realms in Changeling the Dreaming, but that it makes the case that schematics are more fixated, and are shareable for any of their actual mutability strikes me that it hews away from that model (Masterworks are more the crafting thing that leans into mass shared hallucination). </div> </div> </div>I would personally argue that there isn't much of a difference between the two, because obviously the mass shared hallucinations doesn't manifest into reality through the power of faith alone. There is a reason I added the Abyss in there, even the Echo Chamber could easily be replaced by some sort of internet based Infrastructure of some kind, or the titles of a True Fae being embodied by the ON agents as long as they don't violate the Terms and Conditions they clocked on without reading them. I used that angle because I thought it actually gives more power to the hunters compared to the other options. Echo Chamber is, in essence a passive and reactive mechanism- the hunters desire a reality, it works to manifest it under the condition it is not true, and will generate more misinformation. Compare that to a Gentry or a Demon manipulating the conspiracy, and they obviously hold more control in that regard. I also considered some sort of Sekhem Sorcery as well in that regards (again, Visitors), yet I just did the Enigmatics so it felt like to repetitive. <br /> <br /> Also, I would be lying if I were to say that Genius wasn't there at the back of my mind, but I have never actually fully read the game, neither have I played it nor am I a huge fan of it. If anything, as mentioned, the Technocracy was more dominant in my mind in this section (but really, what is the difference :P)<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For whatever else can be said about the overall wibbliness of the conspiracy in terms of of what's going on under the hood for it, one thing stands out pretty strongly-Roy Elliot is a very convienent actor. Foster spends all these years running from pursuants and never getting a clean lead, and then when he meets Elliot, who's as dubious as Marcel's father, all of a sudden the man has compelling enough evidence of an alien invasion with multiple competitors and several high tech facilities that Foster then commits to forging Nebula? That, as the kids like to say, is sus-and more than that, it's shockingly coherent for being so convenient, and it doesn't carry the stink of nihilism that usually surrounds the presence of the Abyss. </div> </div> </div>I honestly mostly disregarded Roy Elliot as a significant factor. He's just, well, there. Obviously will be interested in seeing a cool twist including him (yes, I know you are going with either a Demon or a Changeling, but I'll always vote Visitor if Echo Chamber is not around).<br /> <br /> And yeah, the Abyss and the God Machine do share more in common than they would like to admit. A strong hate towards Hell, for example (and being eldritch entities that contradict reality and all of that jazz)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T13:20:28-07:00","id":1515962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Teatime</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515942#post1515942\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't gotten <i>Tending the Flame</i> yet, so all of this is my introduction to these Conspiracies. I wonder if the original lives up to this thread...<br /> <br /> Anyways, if Xenotechnology truly is created through belief, it will carry some very disturbing ramifications. And I mean that in the more immediate sense than an alien base manifasting in Antarctica. The question to ask is: what is a valid target for extraction? Could an operative hunt down a dog with severe mange, convinced they caught the Chupacabra, and successfully extract a Core from it? If not, what if that same dog was Ghouled by a vampire? What if it was merely controlled through Animalism? What if it was a person controlled through Dominate? What if it was a person with no connection to the supernatural whatsoever? It only takes one act of violent paranoia to kill an innocent person, and have Xenotechnology produce proof of your righteousness. Proof that can be shared with others. And if that person belongs to a group you disagree with, very soon local ON may become the biggest threat to mundane pople, dwarfing even monsters in the shadows.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, you just made it much more terrifying. Thank you for that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T15:12:16-07:00","id":1515965,"title":"","content":"Going by the Exile trying to become a God Machine<br /> <br /> The issue Exiles have is that they are Abandoned. They want purpose and a mission, and without that they are left desperately trying to make one for themselves. Tragically, Exiles are proof that Angels need direction in their lives, what with lacking the rebellion and incremental individualism often necessary for the making of a demon.<br /> <br /> So what if an Exile (maybe one with connected to the internet in some way) stumbled upon the &quot;founding members&quot; of Operation Nebula? Maybe this Exile became enamored with these conspiracy theorists, both because they are &quot;right&quot; in a manner of speaking, but more importantly because they follow screwball logic that nonetheless reminds the Exile of the God-Machine and the workings of its occult infrastructure.<br /> <br /> So that Exile decides to help. In fact, it decides to be the guide necessary for this group to grow. It perhaps hijacks, or becomes, Infrastructure needed to do this. Hell, that Exile was Elliot, and Foster was just the man needed to get the project going. In doing so, the Exile finds itself becoming something more...something like the God Machine. Yet as it grows, it is different in one key way. It grows WITH the humans. It's a larval God machine that's not separate but utterly entertwined with humanity. Which wouldn't be that bad, if it were not connected with the most convoluted and paranoid of them.<br /> <br /> But that theory does lack the thematic of wonder I think can be a possible aspect of Operation Nebula. I look forward to Arc's fae interpretation","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T15:20:05-07:00","id":1515966,"title":"","content":"Well, a Exile trying to become a God Machine is something that I already used to one of my own homebrew conspiracies (Raziel Ltd), so this is already taken for me, but it definitely could be a way to tie ON to the wider CofD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T15:22:31-07:00","id":1515967,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515966#post1515966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, a Exile trying to become a God Machine is something that I already used to one of my own homebrew conspiracies (Raziel Ltd), so this is already taken for me, but it definitely could be a way to tie ON to the wider CofD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool! Can I read that up on here?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T15:37:17-07:00","id":1515971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515916#post1515916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I don't wanna bother with this in talking about ON on the other thread but it's admittedly only tangential here, but whatever: Xenotechnology continues the illusion of a march of things that continues to make Genius less and less relevant, and I am somewhere between very okay and very meh with that..</div> </div> </div> </div>A slight tangent but Genius sort of stopped being relevant when demons introduced gadgets and other things like iron wolves, ordo dracul and or deviants. Though if xenotechnology is created via false information then this pretty close to genius.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":893,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/893-reaperfrost8","user":"reaperfrost8","date":". 06-23-2023, 06:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/893-reaperfrost8","name":"reaperfrost8","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar893_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T15:50:13-07:00","id":1515973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Primordial newcomer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515967#post1515967\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Cool! Can I read that up on here?</div> </div> </div> </div>It is not explicit in the text, but you may find the conspiracy in my sig","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-23T15:55:13-07:00","id":1515974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1515973#post1515973\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It is not explicit in the text, but you may find the conspiracy in my sig</div> </div> </div> </div>Gotcha and thank you","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T12:27:28-07:00","id":1516119,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Survivors Club</b></span><br /> <br /> The Survivors Club could have easily been my favorite conspiracy in the book- they are a very cool organization which takes the abused and gives them power used against their tormentors, their Endowments are incredibly cool, and they really let you play Buffy in the CofD (which the Maiden's Blood Sisterhood have also did, yet to a lesser degree I think). Overall, they are very cool- but like some other organizations in the book, they feel a bit too &quot;compact-y&quot; in presentation, with the main reason for it being, probably, their name. A world wide organization can't use the name &quot;Club&quot; without it undermining its place as a global entity, unless it is added with some occult feature (for example, the &quot;Hellfire Club&quot; or &quot;Hunts Club&quot; could work as a conspiracy name, imo, because it creates a dark &quot;feeling&quot; which the name &quot;Survivors Club&quot; lacks). As such, I would have personally renamed the Survivors Club into something like the <b>Survivors Society</b>, but it is probably not a game changer overall. There are a few other &quot;weak points&quot; which I find in their writeup, but for those I'm going to refer through the text itself.<br /> <br /> While still being relatively &quot;young&quot;, the Survivors are quite old, having around 50-40 years in existence. They have splintered from the Maiden's Blood Sisterhood, which honestly makes sense considering that the two cover similar &quot;last girl&quot; themes (even though they are more flexible towards who they recruit), mostly because they felt the &quot;darkness within&quot; which made them more violent and dangerous compared to other hunters. Then they started reaching towards other people who have had a similar experience, time skip (argh), and they are now a conspiracy! So.. yeah, the main issue with this conspiracy is the said &quot;time skip&quot;- it is not clear how did they became a conspiracy, especially as much of their vocabulary still uses academic jargon, such as &quot;campus&quot; and &quot;advisory council&quot;, but at least we do know how they have access to their Endowments- a parting gift from those who tried to murder them, yet died instead.<br /> <br /> There is no need to beat around the bush- the Survivors all have a little slasher inside. The Mirror Darkly ritual is not a means to get Endowments, but to cultivate them under a strict control. Each member of the conspiracy already had the darkness lurking within them, they only need a way to face them under the comfort and support that their organization offers. That's explicitly mentioned that while the hunters of the Survivors maintain access to their Endowments even without membership, if they are to leave the conspiracy they quickly degenerate into slashers simply because they no longer have the same support network. So while the fact that the survivors have a little slasher inside of them, waiting to hatch (while they work very, very hard to make sure it won't), they interesting thing is <i>how</i> they gain that slasher- by surviving a slasher attack, and specifically, by killing it. And why is it important, you say? Because according to 2e, the CofD is currently suffering from a Slasher Epidemic- and the Survivors Society might have just been the ones to identify the vector.<br /> <br /> While the writeup seems to focus on those who have dealt the killing blow as the &quot;infected&quot;, by the fact that the founders are made of 2 people which have both survived the attack of the slasher and shared responsibility in killing it shows that the criteria is more flexible than &quot;the one to put a bullet in their head&quot;. As such, I think that it should be related to a more nuanced case- the idea of not merely surviving a slasher attack, but also taking up the Vigil <i>as part of the incident</i>. Naturally, in most instances, that would mean hunting the slasher, and generally it would even mean killing it, but it is a more flexible criteria that makes sense especially when we consider the origin of the Code. In many ways, the Code serves as some sort of a &quot;natural defense mechanism&quot; of the human mind- a person stumbles upon something that they can not and will not understand, and gets traumatized by it, yet instead of trying to rationalize the process or deny it, hunters are those where their mind takes a stand against the darkness. Where there is a &quot;fight or flight&quot; mechanism, the Code takes &quot;fight&quot; and turns it into the default process, shaping their whole mentality around &quot;the Enemy&quot;, and it is not a &quot;person. Now, we also know that this shift in mentality may accelerate degeneration into becoming monsters, as slashers are more common among fallen hunters than the general population (that's was especially well defined in 1e's Code, alas we have lost this one). However, let's take everything that we have learned and put it into a different context- what if the Code is not merely a &quot;fight or flight&quot; mechanism on steroids? After all, the human mind is very adaptable, and it is known to shape its identity around trauma. Abusers create abusers, those who experience violence may end up adopting it. Violence forever leaves it mark on the soul, as cognitive patterns try to explain the trauma, give it meaning- and the experience to the supernatural is violent and traumatic by nature. In that case, what if the Code is not, actually, a defense mechanism- but pattern harnessing mechanism? The hunter embrace the Code not because they hate the supernatural, but because their monkey brain takes the trauma and shapes their identity around them. The abused will become the abusers. The hunters see the darkness, and its patterns are embodied within them. Taking the Vigil is not to fight monsters- is to become like a monster.<br /> <br /> Now we are getting somewhere- lets start pushing it forwards.<br /> <br /> So the Code is the process where humans look upon monsters as say &quot;that's horrifying, I should shape around this trauma my all personality&quot;. However, in most cases, this does not lead into becoming a slasher- at least, not immediately, and not as long as their mind is stable. This is different for those that the Survivors recruit- their mind is contaminated by slasherhood from the get-go. The darkness is always found within them, putting them apart from the common hunters. In order to explain this, we need to consider that unlike most other forms of monstrosities, such as vampires and werewolves, slashers do not generally form by supernatural effects- those the mind can't mimic, and as such can not produce (thankfully). Slashers are a different story- the reason that they form is that their mind becomes alternated to the point that they no longer think like human beings, and no longer recognize other human beings as related to them- only then the darkness seeps in and gifts them with their Dread Powers. As such, the very essence of being a slasher is in their behavior- their tells, their targets, their Undertaking, their modus operandi, everything is contained within the psyche. And if the Code is about the hunter getting through trauma which mimics the behavioral patterns of being a monster, and the source of being a slasher is found within the same patterns, then by adopting the Code while surviving a slasher attack, the mind, instinctively, adopts a sliver of the slasher's own entity into it. Their Code becomes contaminated, like by a virus, which means that their own personality now revolves around a trauma which tells them one, simple instinct- kill.<br /> <br /> Again, all hunters share that instinct- it is found within the Code. However, the Code also upholds the separation between a &quot;human&quot; and a &quot;monster&quot;, which is very important considering that it makes sure that the hunter only &quot;kills&quot; who they need to kill. Sure, different hunters may argue about what makes a monster a &quot;monster&quot;, which shapes how they apply the Code- but it is still an essential part of their identity. This is different for slashers, who no longer have that distinction. Heck, their mind becomes so twisted, that the reason that slashers become Soulless is probably because it is just no longer compatible with the human soul. And the Code of the Survivors becomes contaminated by the same, less defined distinction between &quot;people&quot; and &quot;monsters&quot;, giving a slight more advantage to the &quot;kill&quot; instinct. And the &quot;kill&quot; instinct has the tendency to drive the hunters into Breaking Points, which weakens further their ability to distinguish the two, which further increase the &quot;kill&quot; instinct, and so on and so on. I mean, even if you decide that &quot;slashers existing in their patterns&quot; is a reach, the Code being a trauma is very much a canon- and what triggers the Code for the Survivors? The act of defining an entity which, by all external accounts, is a <i>person</i>, as a <i>monster</i>. Slashers are supernatural entities, sure, but the mind can't really pick it up- what it sees is that you <i>killed a human being</i>, and that <i>all human beings could become monsters</i>. Which means that the distinction between &quot;people&quot; and &quot;monsters&quot; must be looser, as they have seen by their own eyes the proof that the two are not that different from one another.<br /> <br /> As such, what the Mirror Darkly ritual does seems to be that it simply allows the hunter to slowly and selectively control their Code- redefine what does it mean, for them, to be a person, and what does it mean for their enemies, the slashers, being monsters. Through careful description of who they are and how they are still human, they can control the corruption within their Code, and stop it from bringing down their whole personality. Without the comfort and help of the conspiracy, however, hunters with contaminated Code are doomed to degenerate into slashers by themselves, which means that they cause more people to experience the Code, which enforces upon them the exact same corruption- hence the epidemic. The Survivors, in that regard, are not much of a vaccine as much as they are a treatment for a chronic illness- you can live with the tendency to become a slasher, you can't get rid of it completely but it is possible to keep it under control and stop it from erupting. As such, in theory, everyone with the tendency for slasherhood, not only hunters, may join the conspiracy- however, they need to adopt the Code in order to keep the eruption under control, and they are harder to be found, or understand that they need to be treated. As such, the vast majority of the Survivors are those who had the contaminated Code in the first place, as they have the highest tendency to fall into such dangerous behavior, and also the ones who recognize the fastest that they may need help.<br /> <br /> While that explains the Endowment of the Survivors, I want to go back a bit to how the Survivors became a conspiracy- again, the name and focus around academic culture is a bit complicated, but after being part of the Debate world I can see, a bit, how it could work- different debate societies are being organized into national leagues, which in turn create international leagues, with their own cultural codes and constitutions and nice things like that (hence a common joke that Debate is basically joining a cult). The problem with that structure is that while it allows a relative quick expansion using university infrastructure, Debate (and, I assume, similar societies) don't really have much of a budget- the university either gives them money or that the members pay by themselves, and a lot of people (such as myself) work at managing the societies and clubs volunteers. As such, while the infrastructure would provide a reach, it lacks the money and resources needed for this expansion (for example, how do you go to your university and explain that you need a budget in order to go around and kill monsters?). As such, it is likely that the conspiracy is likely to both have members from within the university staffs, which makes it very integrated into the academic background, as well as people who complete their degrees and either get integrated in consulting services, support groups or even startup companies (which is probably from where most of the money is going). LinkedIn based conspiracy, more or less. Argh, I really don't like using the internet as an excuse, but in the case of how the academy and money are intertwined, the only way to rationalize it is by turning to a infrastructure which combines the League system, support from academic staff, and LinkedIn. We can only work with what we have, after all. (Note, the main reason for substituting &quot;Club&quot; with a &quot;Society&quot; is how interchangeable the words are in reference for clubs in the debate world). <br /> <br /> Anyway- back to the interesting subject: if the Survivors are indeed people with a contaminated Code which lets them have a nascent slasher in their heart, what is their endgame? What is their &quot;true goal&quot;?<br /> <br /> It is very easy, actually. We have already talked about it. The Survivors are the treatment, but that's not enough to stop the epidemic. <br /> <br /> You need to develop a vaccine.<br /> <br /> As slasherhood is a mental condition, the vaccine itself should also be a mental process- therapy of some sort, using psychoanalysis or CBD or even psychiatry. The mind needs to be coded, or even Coded, in advance, in order to stop the spread. The Survivors work at helping potential slashers to keep them under control- but also study their cognitive patterns, how their psyche works and how it reacts. The Code is a product of trauma, so understanding trauma is essential in order to understand how to stop the infection. It could be a process leading to the creation of a new Code- perhaps one which is unified enough, or one which is more flexible. It may require a mass exposure of the supernatural to the world, allowing people to go through the trauma all at once. Or perhaps a normalization of it- if monsters are viewed as a natural part of the world, they would no longer cause the same trauma, which would lead to the creation of new slashers. Or it could be the identification of the sites in the brain which are related to the soul, and find a method to stop the detachment of the later from the former. And it might just be that there is only one solution to stop slashers from emerging once and for all- the elimination of the Code itself. There are other reactions to trauma, after all, but slasherhood could always trace its origin to this reaction. That in the end, all hunters are monsters, and as the slasher virus lives in the patterns, the Vigil itself must be sacrificed in order to put an end for the darkness once and for all.<br /> <br /> The light may be gone- but it would take the shadows away as well, and isn't sacrifice what the Vigil is all about?<br /> <br /> ********************************<br /> <br /> We are reaching the end, everyone! Only two more conspiracies! I hope you enjoyed it, and next time we'll have the Watchers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-26T14:46:50-07:00","id":1516260,"title":"","content":"Hmm, very interesting take. I do think the odds are stcaked against them even more than other conspiracies. They face a spiritual risk moreso than most conspiracies after all.<br /> <br /> With this end game in mind, do you view the Vigil/Code as a pseudo mystical, outright mystical, or a perfectly mundane facet of reality? Or perhaps a mundane facet that nonetheless carries a magical backing such as an astral representation?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-28T13:41:43-07:00","id":1516524,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Primordial newcomer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1516260#post1516260\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm, very interesting take. I do think the odds are stcaked against them even more than other conspiracies. They face a spiritual risk moreso than most conspiracies after all.<br /> <br /> With this end game in mind, do you view the Vigil/Code as a pseudo mystical, outright mystical, or a perfectly mundane facet of reality? Or perhaps a mundane facet that nonetheless carries a magical backing such as an astral representation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I assume that the Code is as mystical as Humanity, Harmony or Wisdom are- which is not really, but kinda? I mean, the CofD is very much non Physicalist and very much a magical universe. Even the lack of magic is, in fact, a magical process. The Code represents the mind of the hunter and the modifications caused to it by the Vigil, and the mind is the entity which connects the soul (or its equivalent) to the body. As such, the mind is a non physical entity which is related to a spiritual entity which has mystical implications (hence why degeneracy of the mind has impact of the body, as well as how many Integrity equivalents have actual benefits and disadvantages for mystical processes). So Code is mystical by the same standard that the mind is mystical, and that influence the connection between the soul and the body to the degree, which means it is probably possible to be viewed astrally. I personally do not think that the Code instinctively distinguish a &quot;monster&quot; from a &quot;person&quot;, even though that's what 2e Core suggests iirc, and instead view that suggestion as a guideline and not a law, but the fact that the Code outright derives conditions such as Vigilant which modify the hunter's behavior does express that &quot;mystical&quot; role of the mind in the connection to the soul, not to mention the possibility of its being related to slasherhood and other nice concepts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T15:35:41-07:00","id":1516702,"title":"","content":"So after a very long week, we finally reach to our new entry in the thread-<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>The Watchers</b></span><br /> <br /> So let's start with the shallow and less interesting part of this post- the name. Again, I do not really like names which are too mundane or simple. I assume that in a similar manner to the Enigmatics, the choice of such a name was to make them more global and inclusive (as it is very likely they could have just chosen a certain culture which emphasis reincarnation in its metaphysics), yet it still feels a bit too shallow for me. As such, I would probably use the term &quot;Watchers&quot; as a shortcut for a longer title, based on the entity that the Watchers do watch- Sirius, the Dog Stars (or Sothis, as the Deathless like to call it). Se we could call them something along the lines of &quot;Watchers of the [X] Star&quot;, with [X] being... Dog? Demon? Foul? Mad?.. I think that as a tribute to Tarja Turunen, I would call them <b>Watchers of the Dark Star</b>. Yeah, let's go with that one, it sounds cool.<br /> <br /> Now, let's start from the actual lore of the conspiracy- and let's not beat around the bush. We know who the Watchers are and what they hunt- they are Reborn, and their targets are the Deathless and/or Lifeless.<br /> <br /> But are they? And do they?<br /> <br /> Well, let's not get ahead of ourselves. Let's start from the basics.<br /> <br /> The Watchers seem to date all the way back to 1600 years ago, where a certain village of unknown region was attacked by the Arisen, one occultist revealed themselves to be a Reborn, taught other survivors how to become Reborn themselves, and then they started reincarnating throughout the eras, fighting mummies while waiting to the next apocalypse to sweep throughout the world. This is great for two reasons- first, it gives us an actual timeline for their formation, and second, mummy also have a great tendency to keep track of calendars and such because, you know, Sothis and all of that. So even though that we don't really get info about the exact location of the Watchers in that period of time, we can still look around through the Arisen timeline and seek out for proper candidates. Sure, 2e messed things up a bit with the whole &quot;non-linear time&quot; thing and Irem becoming a global, parasitic event which forces its existence regardless of location as long as it did happened when Sothis started it march (hence Iremite Antarctica), but let's still consider Sothis Ascends as mostly canon for the &quot;main timeline&quot;, or else we are going to some metaphysical chaos which we are not prepared to deal with. Let's just say &quot;the timeline follows Sothis Ascends, yet temporal anomalies can and will happen because of the Judges and their annoying lacunae creating actions&quot;. Now that we have a timeline, let's see where it take us to.<br /> <br /> Not to anywhere special, actually.<br /> <br /> 1600 years ago the year was 423 CE. That's 1333 years too late for the Second Turn and 128 years too early for the Third. Considering that 123 is an order of magnitude smaller compared to 1333, it is likely that we are talking about the Third Turn, which, honestly, feels a bit early? I was sure that some Reborn would have noticed the pattern earlier, or create some organization to deal with it (when I made a similar homebrew it traced its origin all the way to Irem, actually)- but it might be the latest reincarnation of the conspiracy or something along those lines. After all, we have around 5000 years at least worth of Arisen involvement, it is more than enough for groups to rise and fall with the flow of time. Anyway, while there are 123 year difference in the timeline, between the fact that time starts losing meaning when you are immortal and the misinformation that hunter conspiracies generally spread intentionally or not, I can accept that they have lost a hundred years or so while counting (or, like, maybe the Watchers got confused between the 5th Century and year 500. It happens to me all the time). Sure, you would have expected from a conspiracy known as the Watchers to keep track of the calendar somewhat more carefully, but well, they are still only human, that's life what can you do.<br /> <br /> Anyway, going to the Third Turn, the consensus among the Arisen is that it was not a fun time period, known by the delightful names of the &quot;Turn of Dusk&quot; or &quot;Year of Plague&quot;. Irem is forgotten, Rome is broken, monotheism is on the rise and cults are in chaos. The Arisen culture has also spread across the the globe, with their presence being felt across Europe, Africa and all the way to India. More than that, during that era a &quot;dark shadow&quot; covered the Heavens- silencing the Judges and weakening Azar, and that shadow seems to have come from the East. Yet during this time, when the Arisen were more free than they have ever been and ever will be, when the eyes of Fate are blinded and the Devourer slumbers, that's when the Watchers seems to rise for the first time. That's... interesting.<br /> <br /> Let's put a pin on that.<br /> <br /> So the Watchers were formed when a village was decimated by the Arisen and a mystic have shared the ability to see through their past lives. That makes the immediate logical leap to &quot;well, than those guys are Reborn, right?&quot;, which, well, has some issues with it- the reason is that being a Reborn is an independent state. It can not be taken from you. However, the rituals that the Watchers use do not do it- instead, they are able to remember their past lives <i>only as long as they are a part of the conspiracy</i>. As such, you may say &quot;well, so maybe then while the original founder of the conspiracy was a Reborn, the rest of the conspiracy does not- and as such the ritual merely allows them to 'awaken' to their previous reincarnations&quot;. Which is a fair theory- but alas, it also fails in the test of reality. Why? Because Time Capsule outright violates that principle- the hunter's future selves can affect their past lives <i>retroactively</i>. That's... weird. That's even weirder as Lives Remembered explicitly mentions &quot;future lives&quot; as entities from which they can derive knowledge. That's <i>beyond</i> what Reborn can normally do- that's some non-linear BS that Immortals generally can't achieve. Somehow, the Watchers are both more and less than the Reborn. Yet, there is also a stronger implication in here- for it is known that from Mummy's perspective, at least, souls generally <i>don't</i> reincarnate. Instead, they almost definitely ends up consumed by Ammut (unless, maybe, they are Awakened, because Watchtowers calling &quot;dibs&quot; on souls is within the Pax Arcana as it seems, and the Diamond Wheel is part of the Guardians of the Veil's symbolism). So not only that they are not Reborn, they also reincarnate instead of getting muched by the Devourer, and have non linear time perspective- not as powerful as the Arisen's, sure, but it's still there. That's.... f-ing wild, by every standard. So what in Duat's burning wastes is going on?<br /> <br /> And what about the fact that we don't have one founder- but two?<br /> <br /> There is a very interesting symmetry in the writeup, you see. A mystic with knowledge of the past, and a scholar with a knowledge of the future. The mystic was a reborn, and she viewed how the Time of Demons have happened again and again and again. The scholar, on the other hand, knew the paths of the stars- and knew exactly when the Arisen are to be awaken once more. Like, I am not very well versed with Mummy's lore, but the fact that Sothis decrees when the Deathless would rise is definitely not a common knowledge. At the very least, this scholar should have been a high ranking cultist, or a sorcerer, or even an immortal! That guy knew what they were talking about, and together with the mystic they gathered the survivors and made a ritual that would allow them to reincarnate and get reminded of their past and future lives as long as they uphold a Vigil against the Demon Star? That also means that those reincarnations need to more or less stumble upon the conspiracy in order to be reminded of their reincarnations- or, that in a way, that they are destined to become part of the conspiracy. Or should I say, Fated? Again, most people do not end up reincarnated- the vast majority, actually. Only a few have this gift to deny the Devourer's maw, so it must be that the same people rejoin the conspiracy again and again, and if there's entity which has the goal of putting the right people in the right place and in the right time, it is Fate. And that means that someone have twisted Fate to make sure that the conspiracy members will reincarnate and be granted access ton their memories as long as they uphold the Vigil. <br /> <br /> And guess what? Sekhem Sorcery can't just do that BS.<br /> <br /> Like, maybe it was once able to do so- yet already in the time of Cleopatra, Sekhem Sorcery have dwindled in power. Common sorcerers can't do it, especially without a sacrifice. Yet you know who generally fuck with Fate the whole time? The Arisen. And you know who have an extensive knowledge of when the Arisen are going to wake up? The Arisen. And you know who have some extensive knowledge about their previous lives and draw upon their memories? The Arisen. And you know who have the ability to experience time non linearly? The Arisen. So yeah, the mystic and the scholar which established the conspiracy seems like that while they may be a Reborn and a sorcerer, it could very well be that they were, actually, Deathless by themselves. That's however, weird- for why would the Arisen turn against their own gods? Why would they turn against their own kind? And even if they want to do so- how could they even do it, as the act would definitely make the Judges angry?<br /> <br /> Because there is a shadow covering the eyes of Heaven.<br /> <br /> Because they can.<br /> <br /> The Turn of Dusk is the time when the One God rose to prominence, when Azar was weak, when the Judges were silence, Fate was blind and the Devourer was asleep. It was a time of heresy- a time when the Arisen were <i>free</i>. And if there was a time for mummies to turn upon their tyrannical gods, it would be <i>then</i>. I mean, think about it- if the conspiracy reincarnates, then you would have expected that both the mystic and the scholar would have remained figures of high importance- not nameless actors. Like, maybe they are- maybe in a C&amp;C style book we would have had some extension on their place and role in the conspiracy, but they might just be Arisen which have turned against the Judges and used the opportunity to activate this powerful ritual, a mini-Rite of Return, that ties souls with the cause of fighting the Judges for all eternity, investing a semblance of their own non-linear perspective in the powerful Utterances which shake the world. If you want it to be even darker, you may even claim that the destruction that came upon that nameless city was in fact a sacrifice made by the Arisen themselves as payment for the ritual- as Sorcery does require sacrifice, after all. And maybe that's a reach- maybe it was indeed the act of a Reborn and a rogue sorcerer, working together to use the deaths caused by a Arisen as a sacrifice to give that semblance of Timelessness to the Watchers. And maybe there wasn't even a sacrifice- Heaven is blind, after all. If there was a time to cheat the rules and steal a bit of eternity it would have been then. <br /> <br /> Ok, so the Watchers are a product of Sorcery used by abusing the Turn of Dusk in order to create a mini-Rite of Return spell which made a group of survivors of a mummy attack (potentially by the mummies who attacked them)into immortals yet only as long as they are devoted to the Vigil against the Judges. Fun. Yet it still doesn't answer how the spell works exactly- I mean, there seems to be some shared collectivity in Time Capsule, and Fate does seem to guide them towards a common goal through the years. That makes me think that the souls share some sort of a collective entity- a Akashic Library of some sort, floating in the Astral or something like that, through which the souls reincarnate and in which the memories of all past and future lives are being contained. Instead of the lone soul of a Reborn being sealed in Sekhem and going through the cycles of reincarnations, there is a collective of souls, perhaps a soulmeld construct which sends slivers of itself to reincarnate across ages. That's how the collective goal is being preserved- the Watchers are part of the same collective soul, and by aligning themselves with its agenda they are granted their memories back. If you want to make it more insidious, you could say that the Library doesn't actually reincarnates, merely granting memories of previous members which are contained within it to those who join the conspiracy and as such the Watchers do not actually access their own past and future selves, but that's kinda annoying and not fun to me- but you do you.<br /> <br /> Anyway, now that we have established how the ritual have acted, who have casted it, and what was the result, we are left with one more thing- what the Watchers truly aim for? Is it really just &quot;fighting during the next Sothic Turn&quot;? Or is it something else? Well, you probably know the answer for that question- you are in a conspiracy theory thread, obviously it is something else. <br /> <br /> They want to end the Rite of Return.<br /> <br /> That's the obvious goal of the conspiracy- Irem parasitically forces itself upon the world, making sure it exists. The Rite endures for all eternity, twisting time itself to satisfy the ever lasting hunger of the Devourer. Breaking the spell will not be an easy act- but it may be possible, if one were to achieve a complete Timelessness, freed from the Judges, and go back to the past of Irem and put an end to the Shan'iatu before they begin their dark project. Before they bargain with Ammut. Before they murder Azar. A world where Will stands triumphs upon the Law. The Akashic Library, after all, already stands near non-linearity in timeline, which is what allows the Arisen and the Judges to parasitically corrupt the world to accept their existence. If it were to reach complete Timelessness, for example by undergoing another Dusk, than it might just be possible. It could be that the Watchers work towards invoking the same power which brought the Dusk and engulf the world in darkness for all eternity, making everyone Immortal and denying the Judges and the Devourer from their cruel acts of Suffering. That would definitely require the Watchers to go back in time and view the acts of the terrible Rite of Return with their own eyes. And perhaps they already did- you see, I am no fool. A forgotten village with no name or home region? That's not a nameless city. That's the Nameless City. What if the Watchers were always Iremite in origin, and that the story we get in the book mixes two facts- the slaughter of the Iremite population as a sacrifice for the Rite of Return, and the awakening of those memories during the Time of Dusk, which allowed those individuals whose souls somehow escaped the ritual through reincarnation to remember the faults that happened to them and stitch up a pseudo version of the ritual in order to reach non-linearity, at least partially, and then use it to slowly create the unified Akashic Library? To create a perfect memory by harvesting the pieces of the original Rite, all while inverting them in order to bring an Eternal Dusk and make reincarnation open for all humanity? Shoot the Demon Star from the heavens and bring an end to the terror of the Judges and their slave-gods?<br /> <br /> And maybe they have already succeeded in doing it, and we just don't know yet?<br /> <br /> Only time will tell.<br /> <br /> ************************************************** ********************<br /> <br /> Well, and that's it for the Watchers. Next we have the last conspiracy and we finally finish this thread- the White Hare Society. See you next time!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T16:17:56-07:00","id":1516710,"title":"","content":"The Watchers are very interesting when assessing their place in the cosmology, but they make me wonder what the Faithful of Shulpae are to them. It seems Fate is against itself","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T19:18:11-07:00","id":1516721,"title":"","content":"Would like to point out that there are explicitly Reborn who reincarnated from future lives \u2014 albeit they're rare \u2014 and they typically get the memories of their past lives back in bits and pieces over time, often through vivid dreams.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T07:44:57-07:00","id":1516777,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1515462-conspiracy-theory-tending-the-fire?p=1516721#post1516721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would like to point out that there are explicitly Reborn who reincarnated from future lives \u2014 albeit they're rare \u2014 and they typically get the memories of their past lives back in bits and pieces over time, often through vivid dreams.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair point, but I still think that that the Watchers tend more towards none linearity (not that they are completely none linear such as the Arisen) from my understanding on Time Capsule and that the idea that they may regain or lose access to their &quot;Reborn status&quot; depending on their membership in the conspiracy/Vigil is very abnormal.<br /> <br /> Anyway, it's time to finish this...<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>White Hare Society</b></span><br /> <br /> One of the cooler conspiracies out there imo, the White Hare Society are claimed to be a &quot;current&quot; incarnation of an older organization formed during Portugal's Age of Discovery- a good time to be a European and a very bad time to be, well, anyone else. The conspiracy seems to be very fractured, being composed of different chapters (like, really- couldn't 2e actually have developed this concept? The idea of &quot;conspiracy regional variants&quot; is really cool but other than a few examples where none actually used the name the concept is just hanging there, and it's so annoying *sad face*). Anyway, it is claimed that the conspiracy have had numerous name changes, with the current one, White Hare Society, getting stuck at around the 1886- which is, like 137 years of consistency, which is not bad. If we consider that the conspiracy have started in around 1420-1500 (like, depending on when exactly they were formed in relation to the Age of Discovery, so let's say around 1450), that's 436 years of them changing names, so I think that no more than 5 name changes have actually taken place for the conspiracy on average. The fact that their history is even more fractured and that different chapters have their own version for it complex things a bit more, but we are going to deal with that. Anyway, we also know that the conspiracy have originally being focused around discovering other unknown lands, but in modern times they are focused on exploring the otherworlds- which is something we should point out in that regards. Why, you ask?<br /> <br /> Because their Endowments are very otherworlds specific.<br /> <br /> Infusions seems like a strange pick for the conspiracy- basically, they use alchemy is order to distill the otherworldly essence of invisible realms and absorb it into their bodies. The First Infusion makes the hunter attuned to the fabric of the otherworlds and give them a special sense for gateways and entities of a chosen world, yet also enforce upon them a consistent madness. While most of the Infusions seems to affect the body of the hunter directly, at least one also targets other entities, which reinforces that most of the alchemical process is by distilling the ambient energies of different realms into liquid form. Considering that those Endowments are both derived and target otherworlds and their entities, it makes it very hard to imagine a version of the conspiracy which does not deal with those worlds as its primary target- and yet, this seems to be the state. As such, either the WHS was a very different organization in the past- or that things are more complicated than they initially seems.<br /> <br /> For example- what exactly happened in 1886, and how it is related to a White Hare?<br /> <br /> I assume that you have probably connected the dots by yourself- but if you didn't, you'll have to wait patiently for just a little bit more.<br /> <br /> Anyway, due to the strange shift in the conspiracy's capabilities, even if not themes, and the fractured nature of the conspiracy, I tend to believe that the WHS have formed not through a singular entity but as a collection of disparate organizations, each pursuing the Age of Discovery in their own way- and, as it seems, across many different cultures. Like, other than the focus on exploration, I don't really see something which screams &quot;Portuguese&quot; through their writeup- not their names, not their symbolism, and not even the year 1886. Like, maybe I am missing something, but hares and Portugal do not seem to have some strong tie to each other. As such, I tend to believe that there were numerous compacts or near-conspiracy compacts (or even an actual conspiracy and numerous other compacts) which have coexisted alongside each other through the Age of Discovery, yet as its decline have started so the power of those hunter organizations. Portugal is commonly being viewed as the one who initiated the period, which makes it the beginning of the WHS- yet it could even be that the original Portuguese conspiracy/compact wasn't even the one to survive to the modern time, and instead the modern WHS is being composed from the organizations growing from its fall, or those who were inspired by them, eventually being collected together and shifting focus after exploration have died out, forming the modern conspiracy in 1886. Yet what exactly have happened in 1886? Nothing in Portugal, especially not something that is related to a White Hare. However, there was an event which was related to a White Hare in 1886- it just wasn't in Portugal. It was in England.<br /> <br /> Alice in Wonderland- the Musical.<br /> <br /> While the book was originally written in 1865 (and the sequel in 1871), the musical was first performed in 1886- a story about a young girl which follows a white rabbit to a psychedelic world, where she never fits and keeps consuming strange potions which adapt her to the insane world she now roams. Basically, the similarity between the WHS and the premise of Alice in Wonderland that it becomes very clear that the conspiracy was heavily inspired by the story, using it as a symbol to gather the disparate organizations under a single flag, creating the multitudes of histories which was converged when the story of Alice became wildly known, and the founders of the conspiracy became inspired by it. At least, that's what I tend to say- but the similarities are <i>too striking</i>. It is one thing to be inspired by Alice in Wonderland to start looking around for new otherworlds, it is another to have their Endowments a direct parallel to the story. Not only that, the one elixir which is not consumed by the hunters creates a living scarecrow- and that immediately brings to mind a different story which is very similar to Alice in terms of a young girl getting lost in a strange otherworld: the Wizard of Oz. The more we look at it, we can see that the conspiracy is less tied to the real life history of the Age of Discovery and more to the strange stories written after that time period. It seems that things are more complicated than they initially looked like- as again, it doesn't make sense to base a whole monster hunting organization over a children story.<br /> <br /> Unless it wasn't a children story.<br /> <br /> I mean, we already talk about a universe where Dracula and Frankenstein are more of documentaries than fictional works- what stops Alice and the Wizard of Oz from being the same? What if Lewis Carroll wasn't just inspired to write the story as a gift for Alice Liddell, but actually documented a experience which she went through, with her either telling him the story or him being a witness for the strange world? What if L. Frank Baum didn't just made up the Wizard of Oz, but have himself went beyond the Rainbow, following the footsteps left by other hunters who fell through the rabbit hole? In that case, the modern WHS, while being composed from the remains of compacts which have risen during the Age of Discovery and declined alongside it, is in fact a English organization formed by following the footsteps of storytellers and poets, and the discoveries they made when they accidently reached beyond the mortal realm and into worlds invisible- or at least, unified by them. Again, each chapter holds its own history, yet they all converged when Carrol's experiences became widespread, drawing those explorers to Carroll who have shared the secrets of his experience with them, including, potentially, the elixirs which formed the First Infusion. Lewis Carroll was a mathematician, after all- what if through his exploration of mathematical theories, he have somehow stumbled upon the mathematical proof for the existence of otherworlds, as well as, potentially, the theory for how to distil their effects to form the First Infusion of the conspiracy? A reach? Perhaps. Am I biased considering that I'm a sucker for Alice in Wonderlands adaptations and reference? Probably. But is it a possibility? Again, this is the CofD- Lewis Carroll being a hunter who discovered otherworlds and disguised his knowledge within a children story and Baum also being part of the same conspiracy after his discover of the Land of Oz is all within the realm of possibilities.<br /> <br /> Ok, so let's look at the timeline- in around 1450~ we have a rise in the number of hunter organizations dedicated for exploration, with the dominant one (the one who was potentially a conspiracy) coming from Portugal. However, eventually, Exploration started declining near 1700~. Many of the organizations collapse, others manage to survive. Britain, being the main colonial power at that time period, hosts the majority of surviving organizations. Then at 1886, the Alice in Wonderland musical starts, hunters which have already shown interest ion Lewis's books start to have many questions about the play- perhaps it was fitting their own pervious experiences, as some have probably stumbled upon a otherworld during their exploration. They reach to Lewis, and manage to convince him to share his theory about otherworlds and how he applied it to open a gate to an absurd reality, as described in the books. They rebrand themselves as the White Hare Society after the White Rabbit character, and they unite together to become a new conspiracy, growing beyond the exploration of distant lands to the exploration of unseen realms, maintaining a close contact with communities of writers who document their experiences as children srtories.<br /> <br /> That, in turn, leads to a very interesting concept- it is already established that the WHS holds a close contact with writers- but what if it goes beyond that? What if the conspiracy as a whole is built around those children stories? I mean, what if publishing and sharing those stories is the way which the conspiracy communicate between the different chapters, sharing information about their experiences and otherworlds in order to preserve knowledge in plain sight? Like, what if it doesn't end with Alice and Oz- what if this includes, let's say, Narnia? Neverland? Maybe even Middle Earth? The Japanese chapter may be very busy, producing every Isekai manga as part of this information network. I mean, what if Welcome to Demon School Iruma Kun is not merely a metaphor about growing up, self discovery, lgbtq people and neurodivergence- but an actual guide for humans who walk to Hell and now need to deal with demons? Sure, this focus around writers may sound close to the Enigmatics, but I would take the WHS over the Enigmatics every day, not to mention that the territory is wide enough in vacuum. The WHS, in this case, is very much interwind into the world of authors and poets, making most of the Seekers Guild, with the Slayers taking the more active role of being the heroes spoken in those stories. Basically, they immortalize the Vigil in plain sight, keeping the knowledge and the warning of the otherworlds alive within the cultural sphere.<br /> <br /> Ok, that's nice and all- yet in that case, what is their endgame?<br /> <br /> It is very simple, actually.<br /> <br /> Discovery.<br /> <br /> Colonialism.<br /> <br /> Monopoly.<br /> <br /> The WHS wants to explore the otherworlds, sure- but it is definitely not their endgame. The conspiracy currently controls the access to the paths for different worlds, demanding payment for such access from other hunters who are interested in such passage. but that's probably not enough for the conspiracy- there are many more worlds to explore, many more entities who seek to control the pathways. The old gods lurk near the gates, seeking entrance, seeking to control the paths- yet the WHS can not let them to do that. The world is theirs to explore, theirs to claim. They should be the ones who control the paths, allowing humanity to take over the otherworlds and shape them in its own image. They will allow humanity to adapt to those new realms, to make sure they are not too big or too small, and establish their own wonderful realms within them- their own emerald cities, with them being as the great wizards who rule without magic, yet equally feared. And anyone, mortal or otherwise, who would want a safe passage through those realms, would be required to pay to the WHS- and all worlds will be tied together, all paths will be connected, and all will be governed by the gatekeepers, the rulers of the realms invisible.<br /> <br /> Alice will go through the rabbit hole once more- but this time, she will be the one to behead the Queen of Hearts and take the throne for herself.<br /> <br /> ****************************************<br /> Aaaaand that's it! Finally finished with Tending the Fire! honestly, it did helped me to form some attachment to conspiracies I less liked, and did helped me to make sense for many of them. I hope you enjoyed it, and I hope you will also be interested in the upcoming Conspiracy Theory thread, where we are going to explore chosen conspirac...<br /> <br /> <i>Knock on the door</i><br /> <br /> Me: &quot;............Come in?...&quot;<br /> <br /> <i>Door bursts open</i><br /> <br /> Mage: &quot;How dare you!&quot; *throws Tome of the Pentacle right to my face*<br /> <br /> Me: *falling to the floor while Mage starts dragging me outside of the Hunt forum*<br /> <br /> Well, it seems I'm going to have to work on my Mage homebrew for a bit beforehand. See you soon!.. Ok, ok, I'm coming! Stop pulling, you'll rip my shirt off!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a>